WEBVTT

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:31.050
<v Ira Glass>Some things in your life you decide you're never going to talk about. For Jules, it was what her youth pastor did to her long ago when she was a teenager in Texas. She tried to put it aside -- tried to move on. But then #MeToo happened with so many more women reporting men who'd assaulted or harassed them.
<v Ira Glass>有些事情，你决定这辈子绝口不提。对朱尔斯（Jules）来说，那是她十几岁在德克萨斯州时，她的青年牧师对她所做的事。她试图将其抛诸脑后——试图继续生活。但后来“我也是”（#MeToo）运动爆发了，越来越多的女性站出来举报曾侵犯或骚扰过她们的男性。

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:50.300
<v Jules>And Matt Lauer had been on the front cover of the USA Today, and I thought, oh my goodness. It inspired me so much that that very day, I wrote to my abuser, who was now a pastor at a megachurch.
<v Jules>当时马特·劳尔（Matt Lauer）的照片登上了《今日美国》的头版，我心想，天哪。这给了我极大的鼓舞，就在那天，我写信给了那个侵犯我的人，他当时正是一家大型教会的牧师。

00:00:50.300 --> 00:01:08.130
<v Ira Glass>He never responded, so she wrote a blog post about it, thinking, you know, maybe 100 people would see it. That was not the moment we were in. It became international news, and because of #MeToo, there were consequences that would have been unimaginable just a year before. The former youth pastor resigned from his job at the megachurch.
<v Ira Glass>他从不回应，于是她写了一篇博客文章，心想，大概会有100个人看到吧。但那时的环境早已不同。这件事成了国际新闻，得益于“我也是”运动，出现了一些仅仅一年前还无法想象的后果。那位前青年牧师辞去了他在大型教会的工作。

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:32.940
<v Ira Glass>Things changed for Jules, too. All of a sudden, lots of women were calling her for advice on how to handle what they were going through, and she found a lot of meaning in those conversations. She set up a nonprofit that funds therapy for women who've been sexually assaulted, but there were aftershocks from her #MeToo moment that she never could have guessed, like with her three daughters. There's this conversation a year or two after she came forward.
<v Ira Glass>朱尔斯的生活也随之改变。突然间，许多女性打电话向她寻求建议，询问如何处理她们正在经历的事情，她在这些对话中找到了许多意义。她成立了一个非营利组织，为曾遭受性侵的女性提供治疗资金。但她的“我也是”时刻也带来了一些她未曾预料到的余波，比如对她三个女儿的影响。在她公开真相后的一两年里，曾有过这样一段对话。

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:41.870
<v Jules>One of them said, mommy, why do you have to be a survivor? And that just broke my heart.
<v Jules>其中一个女儿说，妈妈，你为什么非要做一个幸存者？这话真的让我心碎。

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:44.140
<v Ira Glass>And how old was she when she said that?
<v Ira Glass>她当时多大？

00:01:44.140 --> 00:02:08.380
<v Jules>Probably about six or seven, and I had to explain, sweetheart, I didn't choose for this to happen. I didn't choose to be a survivor. All my girls have seen me in tears over the years because of all this, and they also know that it takes up a lot of time.
<v Jules>大概六七岁吧，我不得不解释，宝贝，我没有选择让这一切发生。我没有选择成为一个幸存者。多年来，我的女孩们因为这一切见过我流泪，她们也知道这占据了我很多时间。

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:12.730
<v Ira Glass>Right, because she sees you counseling other people and talking to people about it, and
<v Ira Glass>对，因为她看到你在为其他人提供咨询，还在和别人谈论这件事，而且

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:32.740
<v Jules>Right. You know, I'll say, hold on, honey, I'm on the phone with a survivor. I've got an advocacy meeting at 1 o'clock or whatever, and so they see it as taking time away from them. But they also see the emotional toll it can take.
<v Jules>对。你知道，我会说，等一下，宝贝，我正在和一位幸存者通电话。我下午一点有个倡导会议什么的，所以她们觉得这占用了她们的时间。但她们也看到了这可能带来的情感创伤。

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:52.520
<v Ira Glass>Five years after the #MeToo explosion, some powerful and well known men lost their jobs. A few went to prison. Over 20 states passed laws to make workplaces safer. Some changed statutes of limitations so people have more time to report sexual harassment and assault. That change, by the way, is why E. Jean Carroll was able to sue former President Trump recently.
<v Ira Glass>“我也是”运动爆发五年后，一些有权势且知名的男性失去了工作。几个人进了监狱。超过20个州通过了法律，使工作场所变得更加安全。一些州修改了诉讼时效，让人们有更多时间来举报性骚扰和性侵。顺便说一句，正是因为这种改变，E. Jean Carroll 最近才得以起诉前总统特朗普。

00:02:52.520 --> 00:03:30.450
<v Ira Glass>Lots of employers adopted new HR policies, introduced more trainings. There was a bump in reporting for a while, but you know what we haven't heard that much about? How this has played out in the lives of the women who stepped forward and went public with their stories. I and a few of my coworkers have been talking to women like that, and one of the things I thought was so striking was the small ways that the aftermath of coming forward continues to pop up in their daily lives. For starters, a few of them told me that any time they meet someone new, even years later, they do this calculation in their head -- what do they know about me? What have they read, if anything? It's stressful.
<v Ira Glass>许多雇主采取了新的人力资源政策，引入了更多培训。有一段时间举报率确实有所上升，但你知道我们还没怎么听到过什么吗？那就是那些站出来并公开自己故事的女性，她们的生活后来怎么样了。我和我的几位同事一直在采访这样的女性，其中一点让我印象深刻：公开真相后的余波，以细微的方式不断出现在她们的日常生活中。首先，她们中有几个人告诉我，无论什么时候见到新朋友，即使是几年后，她们脑子里也会进行一番盘算——他们对我了解多少？如果他们读过什么的话，读的是什么？这让人压力很大。

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:49.630
<v Ira Glass>Take Tanya Selvaratnam. She says she felt tremendous relief when she spoke out in 2018 about her abusive relationship with the New York State Attorney General, Eric Schneiderman, and he stepped down hours after her story came out. But in the time since then, she still gets reminded of it in certain kinds of social interactions.
<v Ira Glass>以坦尼娅·塞尔瓦拉特南（Tanya Selvaratnam）为例。她说，2018年当她公开自己与纽约州总检察长埃里克·施奈德曼（Eric Schneiderman）的虐待关系时，她感到了巨大的解脱，而在她的故事曝光几小时后，他就辞职了。但自那以后，在某些社交互动中，她仍然会被提醒想起这件事。

00:03:49.630 --> 00:04:42.550
<v Tanya Selvaratnam>One example is I had gone on a few dates with a man who was wonderful and so kind, and we got together one night and he asked how my day had been, and there was an essay I had written, which had just come out in Elle magazine. And he asked if he could read it, and as I was saying what I had been doing that day, I realized, oh wait, we haven't talked about my story because it's not something that I lead with, and he wanted to read it. And I said, sure, and so then he looked it up and he read it. He sent me a beautiful note, thanking me for sharing my story and he's sorry for what I went through, and then I never heard from him again.
<v Tanya Selvaratnam>举个例子，我和一个很棒、很善良的男人约会了几次，一天晚上我们聚在一起，他问我这一天过得怎么样，我当时有一篇文章刚在《Elle》杂志上发表。他问他能不能读一读，当我在说我那天做的事情时，我意识到，哦等等，我们还没谈过我的故事，因为这不是我主动提起的话题，而他想读一读。我说，当然可以，于是他就查到了那篇文章并读了它。他发给我一条很温馨的信息，感谢我分享我的故事，并对我所经历的一切表示遗憾，然后我就再也没收到他的消息了。

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:45.510
<v Ira Glass>What did you take from that?
<v Ira Glass>你从中得到了什么启示？

00:04:45.510 --> 00:05:11.390
<v Tanya Selvaratnam>A good guy, but my story made him uncomfortable. Like I hope that people recognize it was a sliver of my life. It was a short sliver of my life, and that there's a whole life before and after that I have forged separate from that sliver.
<v Tanya Selvaratnam>他是个好人，但我的故事让他感到不舒服。我希望人们能认识到，那只是我生命中的一小片。那是我生命中很短的一小片，在那之前和之后，我有完整的生活，是我独立于那一小片之外锻造出来的。

00:05:11.390 --> 00:05:48.140
<v Ira Glass>Calling around, we heard about women switching careers, falling into debt from legal fees, but we also heard specific things that were so individual to each person. An associate professor at Tufts dental school reported a coworker, and then after, she met with her supervisor about it, and the way that the supervisor responded was so upsetting to her that now, the clothes she wore to those meetings. She says she can't wear those clothes anymore or even look at them. One woman, a wildland firefighter with the US Forest Service, told us that she expected after she reported that her bosses would start training the staff differently. They didn't. So now that's something she does for her co-workers -- kind of DIY.
<v Ira Glass>我们四处打听，听说有些女性改行了，因法律费用陷入债务，但我们也听到了一些非常个人的具体经历。塔夫茨牙医学院的一位副教授举报了一位同事，事后她就此事与上司会面，而上司的反应让她非常难过，以至于她在那几次会议上穿的衣服，她说她再也不能穿了，甚至连看都不敢看。一位美国林务局的野外消防员告诉我们，她原以为举报后，老板们会开始以不同的方式培训员工。但他们没有。所以现在这是她为同事们做的事情——算是自助式的吧。

00:05:48.140 --> 00:06:10.970
<v Ira Glass>Another woman, a truck driver in California, Laura Zuniga, came forward with the expectation that something would happen to the man that she reported. When it didn't, when he kept his job, she shut herself in a room for a while, stopped cooking for her three daughters, stopped taking them out, stopped being a mom. They'd come to her door and she'd tell them to go make themselves cereal or a sandwich. She says it especially got to the two older girls.
<v Ira Glass>另一位女性，加州的卡车司机劳拉·祖尼加（Laura Zuniga），站出来时以为她举报的那个男人会受到惩罚。但当什么都没发生，当他保住了工作时，她把自己关在房间里好一段时间，不再为她的三个女儿做饭，不再带她们出去，不再做一个妈妈。她们会来到她的门前，她就叫她们自己去弄麦片或三明治吃。她说这尤其让两个大女儿感到受伤。

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:20.470
<v Laura Zuniga>Now they're very shy. The two older ones are very shy. They don't want to go nowhere. Somebody comes, they ring the doorbell -- they run to the room.
<v Laura Zuniga>现在她们非常害羞。那两个大的非常害羞。她们哪儿也不想去。有人来了，按门铃——她们就跑进房间。

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.840
<v Ira Glass>And they weren't like that before this?
<v Ira Glass>在这之前她们不是这样的吗？

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:29.710
<v Laura Zuniga>No. Somebody came over, they'll come out like nothing. Now they just wait for the people to leave.
<v Laura Zuniga>不是。有人来串门，她们会像没事人一样出来。现在她们只是等着人走。

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:30.280
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:06:30.280 --> 00:06:36.510
<v Laura Zuniga>They stick to themselves. I think they lost maybe some part of their childhood.
<v Laura Zuniga>她们变得孤僻了。我想她们可能失去了一部分童年。

00:06:36.510 --> 00:06:59.710
<v Ira Glass>When Chanel Miller reported what happened to her, she wound up in front of a jury. It was a pretty well-known case. She was known as Emily Doe, and then came out with her name after #MeToo happened. A swimmer at Stanford University named Brock Turner sexually assaulted her while she was unconscious. She'd been drinking at a party. She says the experience of telling that story in a courtroom has stayed with her in a bunch of ways, including this one.
<v Ira Glass>当香奈儿·米勒（Chanel Miller）举报所发生的事情时，她最终站在了陪审团面前。那是一个非常著名的案件。她当时被称为“艾米莉·多伊”（Emily Doe），在“我也是”运动发生后，她公开了自己的名字。斯坦福大学一名叫布洛克·特纳（Brock Turner）的游泳选手在她不省人事时性侵了她。她在派对上喝了酒。她说在法庭上讲述那个故事的经历以多种方式留在了她的脑海里，包括这一点。

00:06:59.710 --> 00:07:31.940
<v Chanel Miller>If it's 10:00 PM in New York, which is pretty early for New York, and I'm trying to decide whether I'm going to take the subway or a taxi home, in order to make that decision, I tell the story of the evening back to myself. So if I went to a book reading and had some champagne, I think, you know, I sound like a reasonable, credible person. I'm going to take a risk and take the subway. The subway is assumed to be riskier than the taxi.
<v Chanel Miller>如果现在是纽约晚上10点，对纽约来说时间还挺早，我正在决定是坐地铁回家还是打出租车，为了做出这个决定，我会在脑海里重温那天晚上的故事。如果我当时去参加了一个读书会，喝了点香槟，我想，你知道，我听起来像个理智、可信的人。我要冒个险，坐地铁。地铁被认为比出租车风险更高。

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:48.180
<v Chanel Miller>If I went to a friend's birthday party and I was wearing a bear costume and drank Hennessy out of a coconut and won a hula hooping contest, I think, you know, that person sounds a little bit more unhinged. I don't feel like explaining that to a jury, so I'm going to take a taxi.
<v Chanel Miller>如果当时我去参加朋友的生日派对，穿了一套熊的服装，从椰子里喝着轩尼诗，还赢了呼啦圈比赛，我想，你知道，那个人听起来有点疯疯癫癫的。我可不想向陪审团解释这些，所以我还是打出租车吧。

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:54.550
<v Ira Glass>Wow. Because you really play out the whole thing in your head as do I want to say this in court someday?
<v Ira Glass>哇。因为你真的在脑子里预演这一切，想着我以后想在法庭上说这个吗？

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:55.180
<v Chanel Miller>Yes.
<v Chanel Miller>是的。

00:07:55.180 --> 00:07:55.690
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:07:55.690 --> 00:08:12.160
<v Chanel Miller>And what's sad about that is that I am not thinking about my own safety. I'm thinking about how to increase my chances of being believed.
<v Chanel Miller>最可悲的是，我考虑的不是我自己的安全。我考虑的是如何增加我被相信的几率。

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:50.540
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, we have the story of somebody who came forward, and what happened to her next has never been told. In fact, she's only been known as Jane Doe, and she's remarkable in a few ways. First of all, she is so young. She's just a teenager, and she spoke out about one of the most powerful people in her state. And in this case, the story that the public knows is very, very different from what actually happened to her. In some ways, the public story is the opposite of her experience of it. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass>今天我们的节目讲述了一个站出来的女性的故事，她接下来发生的事情从未被讲述过。事实上，她一直只被称为“简·多伊”（Jane Doe），她在几个方面都很不寻常。首先，她非常年轻。她只是个十几岁的孩子，却敢于站出来指控她所在州最有权势的人之一。而且在这个案例中，公众所知道的故事与她实际经历的事情非常、非常不同。在某种程度上，公众所知的版本与她本人的经历恰恰相反。来自芝加哥WBEZ广播电台，这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。请继续收听。

00:08:50.540 --> 00:08:53.450
<v Ira Glass>Act One. The Intern.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕：实习生。

00:08:53.450 --> 00:09:06.680
<v Ira Glass>So the person our show is about today was a 19-year-old intern in the Idaho state legislature, and I should say before I go any further that there is no way to discuss what happened to her without talking about sexual violence. Take that as a heads up for what's to follow.
<v Ira Glass>所以今天节目中的主角是一位19岁的爱达荷州议会实习生，在继续讲述之前，我必须声明，如果不讨论性暴力，就无法谈论她身上发生的事情。请把这当作接下来内容的预警。

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:32.550
<v Ira Glass>In 2021, this teenager reported that a state representative twice her age raped her. She came forward with her story, and then so many things went differently than they would have before #MeToo. Idaho's state house had put in place a basic reporting system for complaints like this. The incident was investigated, there were consequences -- all within a month and a half. The house put on a hearing and came to a fast decision.
<v Ira Glass>2021年，这位十几岁的女孩举报一位比她大两倍的州议员强奸了她。她站出来讲述了自己的故事，随后发生了很多与“我也是”运动前完全不同的事情。爱达荷州众议院建立了一套针对此类投诉的基本举报系统。该事件得到了调查，也有了后果——这一切都在一个半月内完成。众议院举行了听证会，并迅速做出了决定。

00:09:32.550 --> 00:09:54.280
<v Sage Dixon>Committee, we have a motion. I will summarize that motion. Our motion is that representative von Ehlinger should be censured with the following restrictions and conditions. One, that he be immediately suspended without pay or benefits. Two, that he must immediately vacate his office in the capitol and not return to capitol grounds for the remainder of the 66th Idaho legislature. And three...
<v Sage Dixon>委员会成员，我们有一项动议。我将总结该动议。我们的动议是，冯·埃林格（von Ehlinger）议员应受到谴责，并附加以下限制和条件。第一，立即停职，不发工资或福利。第二，立即搬出他在议会大厦的办公室，并在第66届爱达荷州议会余下的任期内不得返回议会大厦。第三……

00:09:54.280 --> 00:10:25.650
<v Ira Glass>The committee vote was unanimous. They then called for a vote from the full House to expel the representative. He immediately resigned. So you know, that's change right, post #MeToo? State legislators talked about how proud they were of their ability to do the right thing so quickly. They praised their colleagues around the hearing as masterful. One of our producers, Miki Meek, watched all this happen back then, and she had a question -- namely, what was all this like for Jane Doe? Here's Miki.
<v Ira Glass>委员会的投票是一致通过的。他们随后要求全体众议院投票驱逐该议员。他立即辞职了。所以你知道，这就是变化，对吧，“我也是”运动之后？州议员们谈论着他们对自己能如此迅速地做出正确决定感到多么自豪。他们赞扬同事们在听证会上的表现非常老练。我们的制片人之一，美纪·米克（Miki Meek），当时目睹了这一切，她有一个疑问——也就是说，这一切对于“简·多伊”来说是怎样的？请听美纪的报道。

00:10:25.650 --> 00:10:34.740
<v Miki Meek>I live just south of Boise, and the story of Jane Doe was all over the news. There was a public ethics hearing in the Idaho House. I watched her answer questions.
<v Miki Meek>我住在博伊西市南边，“简·多伊”的故事上了所有新闻。爱达荷州众议院举行了公开的道德听证会。我看着她回答问题。

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:40.980
<v Deputy AG>Did you make a report to Boise Police Department that on March 9th, you had been raped by Representative von Ehlinger?
<v Deputy AG>你是否曾向博伊西警察局报案，称3月9日被冯·埃林格议员强奸？

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:42.150
<v Jane Doe>Yes.
<v Jane Doe>是的。

00:10:42.150 --> 00:11:10.380
<v Miki Meek>The first thing I noticed -- Jane Doe was young. Just a teenager. Just out of high school. They had her behind these giant black curtains up on a dais at the very front of the room. These big black curtains that hung off some tall metal frames were supposed to shield her identity, which was weird because nothing was being done to disguise her actual voice. I couldn't stop thinking, what is happening here? It felt very Handmaid's Tale, like she was on trial.
<v Miki Meek>我注意到的第一件事——简·多伊很年轻。只是个十几岁的人。刚高中毕业。他们把她安置在房间最前方的高台上，后面挂着巨大的黑布帘。这些挂在金属架上的大黑布帘本来是为了保护她的身份，这很奇怪，因为没有任何措施来掩盖她真实的声音。我无法停止思考，这到底是怎么回事？感觉非常像《使女的故事》，好像她是在受审。

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:12.510
<v Deputy AG>Did you tell him to stop?
<v Deputy AG>你告诉他停下来了吗？

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:13.920
<v Jane Doe>I said no.
<v Jane Doe>我说不。

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:16.950
<v Deputy AG>Did you say other things that indicated no?
<v Deputy AG>你有没有说过其他表示拒绝的话？

00:11:16.950 --> 00:11:38.440
<v Jane Doe>Multiple. Multiple different reiterations. I even tried to just convince him to not, telling him I wasn't on birth control and I hadn't shaved myself or -- things to make me seem unappeasable, you know? And he just continued.
<v Jane Doe>说了多次。反复重申了好几次。我甚至试图说服他停下，告诉他我没有避孕，我也没有刮毛或者——做些让自己看起来让人没胃口的事情，你懂吗？但他还是继续。

00:11:38.440 --> 00:11:51.660
<v Miki Meek>So that's a pretty intense thing to hear a teenager recounting behind a black curtain. And then here's the part that really made me wonder what this was all like for Jane. Something happened after she finished her testimony.
<v Miki Meek>所以，在黑布帘后面听到一个十几岁的女孩讲述这一切，感觉非常强烈。然后就是这一部分，真正让我好奇这一切对于“简”来说意味着什么。在她作证结束后，发生了一件事。

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.280
<v Sage Dixon>Thank you, Ms. Doe. You are excused.
<v Sage Dixon>谢谢你，多伊小姐。你可以退席了。

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:58.420
<v Miki Meek>After she exited the room, the mics picked up a faint scream.
<v Miki Meek>在她离开房间后，麦克风捕捉到了一声微弱的尖叫。

00:11:58.420 --> 00:12:00.250
<v Miki Meek>[SCREAM]
<v Miki Meek>[尖叫声]

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:40.450
<v Miki Meek>It was hers. Some of the representatives conducting the hearing looked around in confusion for a split second, and then got back to work calling up witnesses. Watching this back in 2021, just four years after the #MeToo movement broke, I assumed Jane Doe was part of a younger generation of women who were reporting sexual violence. But who knows? She was literally invisible at the hearing. What was it like for her back behind that curtain and what was that scream? I wanted to find out more about what exactly happened to Jane Doe.
<v Miki Meek>那是她的尖叫。主持听证会的一些议员茫然地环顾四周，然后又回到工作岗位，传唤证人。在2021年回顾这一切，也就是“我也是”运动爆发仅仅四年后，我以为“简·多伊”属于年轻一代举报性暴力的女性。但谁知道呢？她在听证会上实际上是隐形的。在帘子后面对于她来说是什么样子的？那声尖叫又是什么？我想找出“简·多伊”到底发生了什么。

00:12:40.450 --> 00:13:02.750
<v Miki Meek>I reached out to Jane Doe through her lawyers after the hearing to see if she wanted to share more. Five months later, I noticed a couple of missed calls on my phone. I called back. It was Jane. She'd been calling me and not leaving messages, trying to reach me out of the blue because she worried I'd try to record our call without her knowing. She said she was feeling paranoid.
<v Miki Meek>听证会后，我通过她的律师联系了“简”，看她是否愿意分享更多信息。五个月后，我注意到手机上有几个未接来电。我回了过去。是“简”。她之前一直给我打电话却不留言，试图冷不丁地联系我，因为她担心我会瞒着她录音。她说她感到很偏执。

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:22.520
<v Miki Meek>When I finally met Jane in person, she was very direct. She'd been seeing all these people talking about her and finally wanted to give her side of it. She had a lot to say about every part of the process that led her up to the seat behind the black curtain. She just turned 20. She was really funny -- a total goofball. She has a membership at a trampoline gym.
<v Miki Meek>当我终于见到“简”本人时，她非常直率。她看到那么多人都在谈论她，终于想给出自己的说法。对于导致她坐在黑布帘后面的过程中的每一部分，她都有很多话要说。她刚满20岁。她真的很有趣——是个十足的“开心果”。她在蹦床健身房办了会员。

00:13:22.520 --> 00:13:24.410
<v Jane Doe>I go like four times a week.
<v Jane Doe>我一周大概去四次。

00:13:24.410 --> 00:13:26.780
<v Jane Doe>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jane Doe>[笑声]

00:13:26.780 --> 00:13:28.190
<v Jane Doe>I love it there.
<v Jane Doe>我爱死那儿了。

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:30.370
<v Miki Meek>Her favorite? The dodgeball zone.
<v Miki Meek>她最喜欢的是？躲避球区。

00:13:30.370 --> 00:13:32.810
<v Jane Doe>And I just love messing up kids.
<v Jane Doe>我就是喜欢折磨那些小孩。

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:34.220
<v Miki Meek>You mean like nailing them with a ball?
<v Miki Meek>你是说用球砸他们？

00:13:34.220 --> 00:13:42.110
<v Jane Doe>I love being a big sister, and I don't get the chance to be a big sister a lot, so when I get a chance to just, like, go annihilate some little, like, nine year olds, I will.
<v Jane Doe>我喜欢当大姐姐，我没太多机会当大姐姐，所以只要有机会去蹂躏一下那些九岁大的孩子，我肯定会去。

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:43.520
<v Jane Doe>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jane Doe>[笑声]

00:13:43.520 --> 00:13:48.230
<v Jane Doe>They're always like, ah, you got me! I love kids, you know?
<v Jane Doe>他们总是说，啊，你打中我了！我爱孩子，你懂吗？

00:13:48.230 --> 00:13:58.880
<v Miki Meek>Jane's a big sister to many younger siblings. She's always teasing them with fart jokes. But the main thing that really stood out to me about her is just how much she loves nerding out about the state legislature.
<v Miki Meek>“简”有很多弟弟妹妹。她总是用放屁笑话来逗他们。但关于她，真正让我印象深刻的是她有多喜欢钻研州议会事务。

00:13:58.880 --> 00:14:04.820
<v Jane Doe>I have pictures and I would love to show you. I always have a bunch of pictures of the state house -- the state house is so pretty.
<v Jane Doe>我有照片，我很想给你看。我总是拍很多议会大厦的照片——州议会大厦太美了。

00:14:04.820 --> 00:14:08.420
<v Miki Meek>She pulled up a photo of the Idaho state flag hanging from the rotunda.
<v Miki Meek>她调出了一张爱达荷州州旗悬挂在圆形大厅的照片。

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:12.050
<v Jane Doe>You got the tree. You got the deer. And I mean, like, I think it's beautiful.
<v Jane Doe>你看这棵树。你看这头鹿。我的意思是，我觉得这很美。

00:14:12.050 --> 00:14:17.390
<v Miki Meek>Her favorite pictures were of her cubicle -- a big wood desk, swivel chair, and her own name plaque.
<v Miki Meek>她最喜欢的照片是关于她的隔间——一张大木桌、旋转椅，还有她自己的名字牌。

00:14:17.390 --> 00:14:26.420
<v Jane Doe>And it was really funny, too. Like, the sergeant at arms put my name in all caps on it because she said then that people would find me easier.
<v Jane Doe>这太搞笑了。守卫把我的名字全部用大写印在上面，因为她说这样人们更容易找到我。

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:27.950
<v Miki Meek>Had you ever had an office before?
<v Miki Meek>你以前有过办公室吗？

00:14:27.950 --> 00:14:32.240
<v Jane Doe>No, I'd never had my own office. I was shook.
<v Jane Doe>没有，我从没拥有过自己的办公室。我惊呆了。

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:34.190
<v Miki Meek>What did you love about that?
<v Miki Meek>你喜欢那一点？

00:14:34.190 --> 00:14:51.590
<v Jane Doe>Honestly, if I will just get straight to really what it made me feel, I felt like I was walking on gold. Like, literally my entire family -- I'm the first one to ever work for the government, right? I mean, yeah, I was just proud of myself.
<v Jane Doe>老实说，如果我要直接说出那一刻我的感受，我觉得自己走在金子上面。真的，我整个家族——我是第一个为政府工作的人，对吧？我的意思是，是的，我只是为自己感到骄傲。

00:14:51.590 --> 00:15:20.870
<v Miki Meek>The capitol had been a surreal place for Jane to find herself in. Just a few years earlier, she was a pregnant high school freshman trying to figure her life out. She started making money by working field jobs on potato and corn farms, and picked up a night shift at Panda Express. As a sophomore, she enrolled herself in a special high school for teen parents and started volunteering in domestic violence shelters. She quickly became the kind of model overachiever who gets voted most likely to become President of the United States.
<v Miki Meek>对于“简”来说，国会大厦是一个超现实的地方。就在几年前，她还是个怀孕的高中新生，试图理清自己的生活。她开始在土豆和玉米农场打工赚钱，还去熊猫快餐（Panda Express）上了夜班。高二时，她为自己报名了一所针对青少年家长的特殊高中，并开始在家庭暴力收容所做志愿者。她很快就成为了那种被选为“最有希望成为美国总统”的模范尖子生。

00:15:20.870 --> 00:15:30.140
<v Miki Meek>When Jane was a senior in high school, she signed up to be a page in the Idaho House of Representatives. She ran errands for a special committee and was celebrated on the local news for it.
<v Miki Meek>当“简”读高三时，她报名成为了爱达荷州众议院的传令员。她为一个特别委员会跑腿，并因此上了当地新闻。

00:15:30.140 --> 00:15:41.090
<v Newscaster>The first teen mom to serve as a page in the state house. Her work in domestic violence programs brought her here to learn more about the legislative process.
<v Newscaster>第一位在州议会服务的青少年妈妈。她在家庭暴力项目中工作，使她来到这里进一步了解立法过程。

00:15:41.090 --> 00:16:08.330
<v Miki Meek>Jane learned the steps on how to make a bill, and for fun, sat in on meetings of the Judiciary and Rules Committee. She liked tracking all the different drafts of bills that got introduced and seeing what happened to them. She'd grown up with an abusive dad and then had a boyfriend who was violent with her. Seeing firsthand just how badly the criminal process worked for victims, she wondered why it wasn't better. And now that she'd seen the work of a lawmaker up close, she decided that's what she wanted for herself.
<v Miki Meek>“简”学习了如何起草法案的步骤，为了好玩，她旁听了司法与规则委员会的会议。她喜欢跟踪所有提交的不同法案草案，看看它们会有什么结果。她在虐待父亲的阴影下长大，后来又有一个对她施暴的男朋友。亲眼看到刑事程序对受害者有多么不友好，她想知道为什么不能做得更好。现在她近距离看到了立法者的工作，她决定那也是她想要的人生。

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:20.360
<v Miki Meek>So after the page program was over, after she graduated from high school, Jane decided she had to get back to the House. She applied for an internship, and to get it, she needed to find a representative who would sponsor her.
<v Miki Meek>所以传令员计划结束后，她高中毕业了，简决定一定要回到众议院。她申请了实习机会，为了得到它，她需要找到一位议员来赞助她。

00:16:20.360 --> 00:16:28.440
<v Jane Doe>So I just reached out to everybody I could that I knew, you know, because you could find their contact online. You can find their email and stuff, so I just was, like, sending emails like, hey.
<v Jane Doe>所以我联系了我能联系到的所有人，你知道，因为你可以上网找到他们的联系方式。你可以找到他们的电子邮件什么的，所以我只是发邮件说，嘿。

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:29.240
<v Miki Meek>What did you say?
<v Miki Meek>你说了什么？

00:16:29.240 --> 00:16:56.000
<v Jane Doe>I was like, Hi, I don't know if you remember me. I was a page last year. I don't know if you remember on your committee -- blah, blah, blah, depending on who I was talking to. But basically, I'm just like, Hey, you know, I'm really interested in coming back this year and learning more. I think I can benefit you and benefit me at the same time. I have a lot that I want to learn. Like, I wanted to be there, you know? Like, I wanted to learn.
<v Jane Doe>我写道，嗨，不知道你是否还记得我。我去年做过传令员。不知道你是否还记得在你们委员会——巴拉巴拉，取决于我联系的人。但基本意思就是，嘿，你知道，我对今年回来学习更多东西非常感兴趣。我觉得我能给你带来帮助，同时也让自己获益。有很多我想学的东西。我是说，我想在那里，你懂吗？我想学习。

00:16:56.000 --> 00:17:23.540
<v Miki Meek>She got an internship with a Republican representative -- not von Ehlinger, the one she reported for raping her. It was another one. She started at the beginning of 2021 -- a desk of her own on the ground floor of the Idaho State Capitol. She felt like this was the beginning of something much bigger.
<v Miki Meek>她获得了一位共和党议员的实习机会——不是那个被她举报强奸的冯·埃林格。是另一位。她于2021年初开始工作——在爱达荷州议会大厦的一楼拥有了自己的办公桌。她觉得这是某样更宏大事业的开端。

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:46.240
<v Miki Meek>Jane remembers running into Representative von Ehlinger pretty soon after the 2021 legislative session started. She wasn't interning for him. He was just another Republican representative -- a very new one. I reached out to von Ehlinger and his lawyer for comment, but didn't get a response. He hasn't been talking to the press. It was his first term in the House. He was a substitute teacher from Northern Idaho.
<v Miki Meek>“简”记得2021年立法会议开始后不久就遇到了冯·埃林格议员。她并不是他的实习生。他只是另一位共和党议员——一位非常新的议员。我联系了冯·埃林格及其律师请求置评，但没有得到回复。他一直没有接受媒体采访。这是他在众议院的第一任期。他是北爱达荷州的一名代课老师。

00:17:46.240 --> 00:18:14.380
<v Jane Doe>There was a few times when I'd walk out of committee and he was, like, there and I'd just -- you know, I smile and wave at everybody. And then he just kind of stared at me, and I was like, I'm just going to keep walking. And then I think the second time -- there was a bunch of people like right where the entrance was to go get to my office. There was a bunch of people there, so I'm just kind of like lingering, and then Ehlinger was like, Oh, how are you? I had my intern badge, and he was just like, Who do you intern for? And I was like, Well, I'm just, I have my own office over here, and --
<v Jane Doe>有几次我走出委员会，他就站在那里，我只是——你知道，我微笑着向大家挥手。然后他就一直盯着我，我心想，我还是继续走吧。然后我想第二次——入口处聚集了一堆人，那是通往我办公室的必经之路。那里有一堆人，所以我只是徘徊了一下，然后埃林格说，噢，你好吗？我挂着实习生的徽章，他就直接问，你为谁实习？我说，嗯，我有自己的办公室在这里，然后——

00:18:14.380 --> 00:18:17.010
<v Miki Meek>He was holding some cookies shaped like Idaho.
<v Miki Meek>他手里拿着几个爱达荷州形状的饼干。

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:27.130
<v Jane Doe>I was like, oh, those are cool-looking cookies. And he was like, oh, do you want them? And I was like, Sure. Like, I'm just going to my office right now. I'll put them there. And he's like, OK, and just like follows me back.
<v Jane Doe>我说，噢，这些饼干看起来很酷。他说，噢，你想要吗？我说，当然。就像，我正要去办公室，我会把它们放在那儿。他说，好吧，然后就跟着我回去了。

00:18:27.130 --> 00:18:28.670
<v Miki Meek>What did you make of that at the time?
<v Miki Meek>你当时怎么看这件事？

00:18:28.670 --> 00:18:41.000
<v Jane Doe>Well, I mean, I wasn't too weirded out by it. I was just like, OK, like it's just another one of them. Whatever, it's just another sir. There's a lot of sirs in there, OK. There's sirs everywhere.
<v Jane Doe>嗯，我的意思是，我并没有觉得太奇怪。我只是想，好吧，这只是他们中的另一个。没关系，只是另一个先生。那儿有很多先生，好吗。到处都是先生。

00:18:41.000 --> 00:18:41.450
<v Miki Meek>S-I-R?
<v Miki Meek>S-I-R（先生）？

00:18:41.450 --> 00:18:43.700
<v Jane Doe>Yeah, sir. It's just because you've got to say sir.
<v Jane Doe>对，先生。就因为你得称呼别人为先生。

00:18:43.700 --> 00:18:45.810
<v Miki Meek>How would you define what a sir is?
<v Miki Meek>你会如何定义什么是“先生”？

00:18:45.810 --> 00:18:49.670
<v Jane Doe>Like literally suits -- like men in suits.
<v Jane Doe>就像字面意思上的西装——穿西装的男人们。

00:18:49.670 --> 00:19:16.550
<v Miki Meek>Jane adds that sir is the kind of male representative who would text her late at night about a non-urgent work question, who'd keep her in his office too long, and who she overheard talking with other sirs about whether she looked better in her tan skirt or her striped one. She told me there were a lot of sirs in the House. Sometimes she'd get this creeping feeling that they would look at her and think, wow, how exotic. Jane's Hispanic. Many of the other women working in the capitol were blonde and white.
<v Miki Meek>“简”补充说，所谓“先生”就是那种会在深夜给她发无关紧要的工作短信的男议员，会把她长时间留在办公室里，她还会听到他们和其他“先生”谈论她穿那条棕褐色裙子还是条纹裙子更好看。她告诉我，议会里有很多“先生”。有时她会产生一种毛骨悚然的感觉，觉得他们看着她时心想，哇，真有异国情调。“简”是拉丁裔。国会大厦里许多其他女性是白人金发。

00:19:16.550 --> 00:19:34.130
<v Miki Meek>After von Ehlinger gave her those Idaho-shaped cookies, Jane says he was suddenly walking by her desk multiple times a day to make small talk. He gave her his phone number. Food seemed to be the main currency of flirting for a lot of the sirs. Jane says they were constantly offering her snacks from their mini fridges or dropping off lunch.
<v Miki Meek>冯·埃林格给她那些爱达荷州形状的饼干后，“简”说他突然每天几次走过她的办公桌寒暄。他给了她电话号码。对于很多“先生”来说，食物似乎是调情的主要货币。简说他们不断地从迷你冰箱里拿零食给她，或者送来午餐。

00:19:34.130 --> 00:20:01.470
<v Miki Meek>At times, all this free food was nice. Jane's internship was unpaid. She was supporting herself and her three-year-old son with a second job. She worked the overnight shift at a domestic violence shelter for $8 an hour. When von Ehlinger eventually asked her out to dinner, Jane says she initially felt reluctant, but talked about it with her friends, who told her to go for the food. Remember, she was 19 -- only a year out of high school. He was 38.
<v Miki Meek>有时，这些免费食物确实不错。简的实习是没有工资的。她靠一份兼职工作抚养自己和三岁的儿子。她在一家家庭暴力收容所上夜班，每小时8美元。当冯·埃林格最终约她出去吃饭时，“简”说她起初感到勉强，但和朋友们聊了聊，她们告诉她为了食物去吧。记住，她当时才19岁——高中刚毕业一年。他38岁。

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:06.710
<v Miki Meek>Did you think of it as like, this is a date? This is a working dinner? This is something in between or who knows?
<v Miki Meek>你当时觉得这是一次约会吗？这是一次工作晚餐？或者是介于两者之间？

00:20:06.710 --> 00:20:29.980
<v Jane Doe>I obviously knew that he was interested in me, but I more or less was like, well, he's going to help me out. Like, because I told him all my interests, right? Like, I was like, I want to work on the judiciary system, blah, blah, blah, and he's like, you know, I know these people and I could maybe, you know, connect you with, like, he was, no, he didn't, like, promise anything, but he definitely was like, hey, I could help you out. And that was my goal.
<v Jane Doe>我显然知道他对我有兴趣，但我更多的是觉得，好吧，他会帮我一把。因为我告诉了他我所有的兴趣，对吧？我当时说，我想研究司法系统，巴拉巴拉，他说，你知道，我认识这些人，我也许可以，你知道，把你介绍给——不，他没有承诺什么，但他确实说，嘿，我可以帮你。那就是我的目标。

00:20:29.980 --> 00:20:41.210
<v Miki Meek>She wanted to grow her network. Von Ehlinger picked Jane up after she'd finished her internship for the day in a BMW he borrowed from his roommate, then drove her to a steak restaurant downtown.
<v Miki Meek>她想扩展自己的人脉。冯·埃林格在“简”结束当天实习工作后，开着他从室友那里借来的宝马车接了她，然后带她去了市中心的一家牛排馆。

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:48.080
<v Jane Doe>And then over dinner, he had made some weird remarks, and I was like, this is kind of weird.
<v Jane Doe>然后在晚餐时，他说了一些奇怪的言论，我心想，这有点奇怪。

00:20:48.080 --> 00:20:49.940
<v Miki Meek>What kind of remarks struck you as weird?
