<v Miki Meek>Once the hearing was announced, one of von Ehlinger's attorneys did something that shocked Jane and her lawyers -- he released Jane's name to reporters. It ended up on a far right news site with her photo. Jane was no longer anonymous.
<v Miki Meek>听证会宣布后，冯·埃林格的一位律师做了一件震惊“简”及其律师的事——他向记者公布了“简”的名字。它最终出现在一个极右翼新闻网站上，并附上了她的照片。简不再匿名了。

00:32:21.620 --> 00:32:38.340
<v Jane Doe>I'm just like, holy shit, my world is ending. Nobody would talk to me. Nobody would even look at me in the eye anymore. I went from smiling and waving to everybody to nobody would look at me. And so then that's when I was really like, dang, they hate me. Like everybody in that state house just hates me.
<v Jane Doe>我只是觉得，天呐，我的世界要结束了。没人愿意跟我说话。甚至没人愿意再看着我的眼睛。我从微笑着向大家挥手变成了没人愿意看着我。所以我那时真的觉得，该死，他们恨我。就像国会大厦里的每一个人都恨我。

00:32:38.340 --> 00:33:05.540
<v Miki Meek>An ally of von Ehlinger in the House went after Jane, too -- a then representative named Priscilla Giddings. She posted Jane's photo on Facebook and emailed a newsletter to her constituents calling von Ehlinger Idaho's very own Kavanaugh and Jane a honey trap out on a liberal smear job. The hearing, she said, was part of a national #MeToo witch hunt. I reached out to Giddings, but she didn't respond.
<v Miki Meek>冯·埃林格在众议院的一个盟友也针对了“简”——时任议员普里西拉·吉丁斯（Priscilla Giddings）。她在Facebook上发布了“简”的照片，并向选民发送了一封通讯，称冯·埃林格是爱达荷州自己的卡瓦诺，而“简”是一个自由派抹黑行动中的“美人计”。她说，听证会是全国性“我也是”女巫狩猎行动的一部分。我联系了吉丁斯，但她没有回应。

00:33:05.540 --> 00:33:24.530
<v Miki Meek>Jane tried to keep going to work, but she hated that every time someone looked at her, they thought about the rape. She couldn't function and needed to get out of the capitol. Back at home, all she could do to get through the day was sleep. She couldn't keep food down and was failing all of her college classes except for one -- a criminal justice course.
<v Miki Meek>“简”试图继续工作，但她恨透了每次有人看着她，他们想到的都是那场强奸。她无法正常工作，需要离开国会大厦。回到家里，她能度过一天的唯一办法就是睡觉。她吃不下东西，所有的大学课程都挂了，只有一门除外——一门刑事司法课程。

00:33:24.530 --> 00:33:25.520
<v Jane Doe>It was victimology.
<v Jane Doe>那是受害者学。

00:33:25.520 --> 00:33:28.100
<v Jane Doe>[LAUGHTER]
<v Jane Doe>[笑声]

00:33:28.100 --> 00:33:35.060
<v Jane Doe>I'm not even kidding. I'm not even kidding. It was victimology. I was taking victimology all through that.
<v Jane Doe>我不是在开玩笑。我真没开玩笑。那是受害者学。我在经历这一切的同时正在修受害者学。

00:33:35.060 --> 00:34:17.300
<v Miki Meek>In victimology, one of the things they talk about is how victims are often re-traumatized by the very systems that are supposed to get them justice. If you're a victim of sexual violence, the trauma is about having no power, so any attempt toward justice -- police, court, or HR procedures -- should be designed to make sure the person feels a sense of control. For instance, before a victim even makes a report, they should know what all the possible steps of the process could be. That way, they can decide if they still want to proceed. And if it's a workplace like the state house, you want to give the victim the option to give their testimony in private instead of forcing them to retell and relive their assault in public.
<v Miki Meek>在受害者学中，他们谈论的一件事是受害者如何被原本应该为他们伸张正义的制度所“二次创伤”。如果你是性暴力的受害者，创伤在于失去权力，所以任何寻求正义的尝试——警察、法院或人事程序——都应该旨在确保当事人感到有掌控感。例如，在受害者举报之前，她应该知道整个过程中所有可能的步骤。这样，她们才能决定是否还要继续。如果是像国会大厦这样的工作场所，你希望给受害者提供私下作证的选择，而不是强迫她们在公开场合重述和重温她们的攻击经历。

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:40.430
<v Miki Meek>In the Idaho House, the process wasn't designed with the sexual assault victim in mind. Instead, it looked a lot more like a regular criminal trial. Dixon talked a lot about von Ehlinger's right to defend himself, while Jane says she wasn't consulted about whether she even wanted to come to the hearing. They issued a subpoena for her to appear. I asked Dixon why he thought that was necessary.
<v Miki Meek>在爱达荷州众议院，这个过程在设计时并没有考虑到性侵受害者。相反，它看起来更像是一场普通的刑事审判。迪克森谈了很多关于冯·埃林格为自己辩护的权利，而“简”则说没人问过她是否愿意来到听证会。他们发出了传票要她出席。我问迪克森为什么认为这有必要。

00:34:40.430 --> 00:34:49.510
<v Sage Dixon>We were subpoenaing other witnesses, and so to maintain that consistency, I think, is why, and just that it didn't look like we were favoring one side or the other.
<v Sage Dixon>我们正在传唤其他证人，所以为了保持这种一致性，我想这就是原因，而且这样看起来我们没有偏袒任何一方。

00:34:49.510 --> 00:34:56.110
<v Miki Meek>Because it is a subpoena, if she hadn't appeared, then arrest is the consequence of that, right?
<v Miki Meek>因为这是传票，如果她没有出现，那么后果就是被捕，对吧？

00:34:56.110 --> 00:35:03.460
<v Sage Dixon>Yes. I don't think it's absolute that arrest is a consequence of that. If we wanted to take it to that end, we could have.
<v Sage Dixon>是的。我不认为被捕是必然后果。如果我们想采取那种手段，我们是可以的。

00:35:03.460 --> 00:35:06.040
<v Miki Meek>So if she hadn't come, I mean, what would have --
<v Miki Meek>所以如果她没来，我的意思是，会发生什么——

00:35:06.040 --> 00:35:15.960
<v Sage Dixon>I don't know, Miki. That's tough because I feel she needed to be there for the face-your-accuser portion of it.
<v Sage Dixon>我不知道，美纪。那很难，因为我觉得对于“面对你的原告”这一部分，她需要出现在那里。

00:35:15.960 --> 00:35:38.220
<v Miki Meek>In the days leading up to the hearing, Jane's lawyer said they did everything they could to prevent the hearing from being a disaster for her. They asked if she could testify remotely and with her voice disguised, or even better, in a closed session that wasn't open to the public. They said the answer they always got back from the Attorney General's office was no. No one seems to have a clear answer on why this was.
<v Miki Meek>在听证会前的几天里，“简”的律师说他们竭尽所能防止听证会成为她的灾难。他们询问她是否可以远程作证并掩盖声音，或者更好的是，在不对公众开放的闭门会议中作证。他们说，从总检察长办公室得到的回复总是“不”。没有人能给出明确的答案为什么。

00:35:38.220 --> 00:36:05.520
<v Miki Meek>One of the Deputy AG's running the case declined to comment. Dixon told me he never received her attorney's requests and says he would have been open to Jane testifying remotely. In the end, Jane's lawyer said the only protection that was offered up to her were those black curtains. She would tell her story in public, behind a black curtain.
<v Miki Meek>负责此案的一位副总检察长拒绝置评。迪克森告诉我，他从未收到她律师的请求，并说他会支持“简”远程作证。最终，“简”的律师说提供给她的唯一保护措施就是那些黑布帘。她将在公开场合，在黑布帘后面讲述她的故事。

00:36:05.520 --> 00:36:18.550
<v Ira Glass>Miki Meek. Coming up -- the hearing and the protective power of tennis shoes. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>美纪·米克。稍后——听证会以及网球鞋的保护力量。芝加哥公共广播电台的节目将在稍后继续。

00:36:18.550 --> 00:36:31.840
<v Ira Glass>This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today's program -- Jane Doe. Five years since #MeToo blew up and there are all these systems that are supposed to crank into action now when somebody steps forward, we have the story of Jane Doe, a teenager in Idaho.
<v Ira Glass>这里是《美国生活》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。今天的节目——简·多伊。“我也是”运动爆发五年了，现在有各种系统，当有人站出来时，这些系统就应该启动，我们有“简·多伊”的故事，一位在爱达荷州的青少年。

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:58.810
<v Ira Glass>Now to be clear, Jane Doe did not come forward because of #MeToo. Actually, when our producer, Miki Meek, asked her about it, Jane said all she knew about #MeToo is that it was something for celebrities. Nonetheless, the response to her was shaped by #MeToo. She was a test case of the legislature's new reporting system that they were making up as they went. We pick up our story with Act Two, The Witness. Heads up, this part of the story contains brief references to suicide. Here's Miki.
<v Ira Glass>现在需要说明的是，“简·多伊”并不是因为“我也是”运动才站出来的。事实上，当我们的制片人美纪·米克问起时，“简”说她对“我也是”运动的了解仅限于那是一些名人的事情。尽管如此，对她的反应确实受到了“我也是”运动的影响。她是议会新举报系统的测试案例，他们边做边改。我们进入第二幕，证人。预警：这部分故事包含关于自杀的简要提及。请听美纪的报道。

00:36:58.810 --> 00:37:20.370
<v Miki Meek>The morning of the hearing, Jane woke up terrified. She had a full blown panic attack and threw up. She jumped in the shower and put on "Beautiful" by Eminem to try and pump herself up. She then put on a black dress. This was an inside joke to herself because she felt like she was about to attend her own funeral. Jane's mom picked her up and they headed to the capitol.
<v Miki Meek>听证会那天早上，简在恐惧中醒来。她惊恐发作，吐了。她跳进淋浴间，放了阿姆的《Beautiful》来给自己打气。然后她穿上一件黑色连衣裙。这是她给自己开的一个内部玩笑，因为她觉得她快要参加自己的葬礼了。简的妈妈接上她，他们前往国会大厦。

00:37:20.370 --> 00:37:45.360
<v Miki Meek>Her lawyers told me that nobody came up with a secure plan to get her into the building without people seeing who she was. So one of her lawyers reached out to a senator who met Jane and her mom at a private entrance outside the capitol, then led them through a series of tunnels and some back hallways until Jane finally got to a door in the hearing room. She looked at the curtains and a random bronze statue of Abraham Lincoln's head. Her chair was right in front of him.
<v Miki Meek>她的律师告诉我，没人制定一个安全的计划把她带进大楼而不被别人看到她的身份。所以她的一位律师联系了一位参议员，他在国会大厦外的私人入口与“简”和她妈妈见面，然后引导他们穿过一系列隧道和后走廊，直到“简”终于到达了听证室的一扇门前。她看着帘子和一尊随机的亚伯拉罕·林肯头部铜像。她的椅子就在他面前。

00:37:45.360 --> 00:37:53.700
<v Jane Doe>His eyes wouldn't move, you know? He was just staring at me the whole time, and I was like, OK. It was just weird. It was just flat out weird.
<v Jane Doe>他的眼睛不会动，你知道吗？他一直在盯着我，我心想，好吧。这简直太诡异了。简直怪透了。

00:37:53.700 --> 00:37:56.160
<v Miki Meek>What did you think when you saw that black curtain?
<v Miki Meek>当你看到那块黑布帘时，你觉得怎样？

00:37:56.160 --> 00:38:05.460
<v Jane Doe>Well, I was like, I can see through this. I can see everybody. Like, they can see me, and I was kind of freaking out at first, but they really couldn't see me.
<v Jane Doe>嗯，我心想，我能看穿这个。我能看到所有人。就像，他们能看到我，我起初有点崩溃，但他们实际上看不到我。

00:38:05.460 --> 00:38:34.890
<v Miki Meek>From her side, the curtain looked more sheer. She looked through it to a room packed with legislators and press. The people who could actually see her, though, included five Ethics Committee members, their counsel, a secretary, two Deputy AG's, von Ehlinger's attorney, and von Ehlinger. Jane had only seen him around the capitol a couple of times since he raped her. Her black curtain was slightly open to give those people line of sight on Jane during the hearing.
<v Miki Meek>从她那边看，帘子看起来更透明。她透过帘子看向一个挤满了议员和媒体的房间。然而，真正能看到她的人包括五名道德委员会成员、他们的律师、一名秘书、两名副总检察长、冯·埃林格的律师和冯·埃林格。自强奸发生后，“简”在国会大厦只偶尔见过他几次。她的黑布帘被稍微拉开，以便在听证会期间让那些人能看到“简”。

00:38:34.890 --> 00:38:44.400
<v Jane Doe>My body was not there. I just held so tight to my chair -- just held on to it just so I had something.
<v Jane Doe>我的身体不在这儿。我只是紧紧地抓住我的椅子——紧紧抓住它，只是为了有东西抓着。

00:38:44.400 --> 00:38:46.590
<v Miki Meek>And you knew they weren't going to protect your voice going into it?
<v Miki Meek>而且你进入之前就知道他们不会保护你的声音？

00:38:46.590 --> 00:38:48.810
<v Jane Doe>No.
<v Jane Doe>不知道。

00:38:48.810 --> 00:38:53.130
<v Miki Meek>Jane's voice was live-streamed. The hearing was broadcast online.
<v Miki Meek>“简”的声音被直播了。听证会在网上播放。

00:38:53.130 --> 00:38:56.490
<v Edward Dindinger>I think we need to be able to cross-examine the accuser here.
<v Edward Dindinger>我认为我们需要能够在这里盘问原告。

00:38:56.490 --> 00:39:17.700
<v Miki Meek>That's von Ehlinger's attorney. Publicly cross-examining witnesses of sexual assault is certainly not best practice for this kind of thing. For instance, other state legislatures that have adopted procedures for handling complaints do not allow victims to be cross-examined in public, certainly not by the perpetrators. But again, they had no process for this in Idaho.
<v Miki Meek>那是冯·埃林格的律师。公开盘问性侵受害者绝对不是此类事情的最佳实践。例如，其他采用投诉处理程序的州立法机构不允许受害者在公开场合接受盘问，绝对不允许被施暴者盘问。但同样，爱达荷州对此没有任何流程。

00:39:17.700 --> 00:39:48.360
<v Miki Meek>So von Ehlinger's lawyer adopted tactics out of a criminal trial. He predictably turned Jane into the defendant, following the usual playbook that gets used on sexual assault victims. He cast her as mentally unstable and a flirt, which was a thing Jane had felt around the capitol ever since she reported von Ehlinger. That she was being judged and cast, in her words, as a, quote, "gold digging, crazy Mexican pothead." Von Ehlinger's lawyer nitpicked tiny details to insinuate that maybe she was a liar.
<v Miki Meek>所以冯·埃林格的律师采取了刑事审判的策略。他不出所料地把“简”变成了被告，遵循了通常用来对付性侵受害者的剧本。他把她描绘成精神不稳定和爱调情的人，这是“简”自从举报冯·埃林格以来在国会大厦一直能感觉到的东西。她正在受到评判，正如她所说的，被定型为“贪财、疯狂的墨西哥瘾君子”。冯·埃林格的律师吹毛求疵，挑剔微小的细节来暗示她可能是个骗子。

00:39:48.360 --> 00:39:52.140
<v Edward Dindinger>I'm just wondering if you can just read that -- just that one sentence.
<v Edward Dindinger>我只是想问你能不能读一下——就那一个句子。

00:39:52.140 --> 00:39:56.340
<v Jane Doe>On the night of March 10, 2021, she met him in downtown Boise.
<v Jane Doe>2021年3月10日晚，她在博伊西市中心见到了他。

00:39:56.340 --> 00:39:59.300
<v Edward Dindinger>Thank you. But it wasn't March 10, 2021, was it?
<v Edward Dindinger>谢谢。但不是2021年3月10日，对吧？

00:39:59.300 --> 00:40:00.060
<v Jane Doe>No.
<v Jane Doe>不是。

00:40:00.060 --> 00:40:01.910
<v Edward Dindinger>It was March 9th. Isn't that correct?
<v Edward Dindinger>是3月9日。没错吧？

00:40:01.910 --> 00:40:03.510
<v Jane Doe>Yes, I told police that.
<v Jane Doe>是的，我告诉警察了。

00:40:03.510 --> 00:40:06.260
<v Edward Dindinger>OK, why did you tell Miss Blackburn that it was March 10?
<v Edward Dindinger>好吧，你为什么告诉布莱克本小姐那是3月10日？

00:40:06.260 --> 00:40:08.170
<v Jane Doe>I didn't. I said the other night.
<v Jane Doe>我没说。我说的是那天晚上。

00:40:08.170 --> 00:40:43.140
<v Miki Meek>The supervisor who Jane had reported to is the one who accidentally made a mistake in her written report, not Jane. Meanwhile, von Ehlinger barely spoke. He kept pleading the fifth. When the Ethics Committee kept pointing out that this wasn't a criminal proceeding and it didn't apply, he still refused. What sucked up most of the time, though, was the core of von Ehlinger's defense, which was essentially, this was not rape -- we were dating, which is allowed. There's no anti-fraternization policy in the Idaho state house. Again, here's his attorney.
<v Miki Meek>“简”向其举报的主管才是那个在书面报告中意外犯错的人，而不是“简”。同时，冯·埃林格几乎没怎么说话。他一直保持沉默。当道德委员会一再指出这不是刑事诉讼，且不需要那样做时，他仍然拒绝。然而，耗费大部分时间的是冯·埃林格辩护的核心，本质上就是：这不是强奸——我们在约会，这是允许的。爱达荷州议会没有反办公室恋情政策。再次，请听他律师的话。

00:40:43.140 --> 00:40:47.880
<v Edward Dindinger>Does Representative von Ehlinger have any hiring or firing authority over you?
<v Edward Dindinger>冯·埃林格议员对你有任何聘用或解雇的权力吗？

00:40:47.880 --> 00:40:49.530
<v Jane Doe>No.
<v Jane Doe>没有。

00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:53.750
<v Edward Dindinger>Does Representative von Ehlinger have any supervisory authority over you?
<v Edward Dindinger>冯·埃林格议员对你有任何监督权力吗？

00:40:53.750 --> 00:40:54.640
<v Jane Doe>No.
<v Jane Doe>没有。

00:40:54.640 --> 00:40:55.530
<v Edward Dindinger>OK.
<v Edward Dindinger>好的。

00:40:55.530 --> 00:40:59.700
<v Miki Meek>He asked some version of this question more than 30 times to different witnesses.
<v Miki Meek>他向不同的证人问了这个问题不下30遍。

00:40:59.700 --> 00:41:11.290
<v Edward Dindinger>Are you aware of any policy that would be violated by a representative having a relationship with an intern of the age of majority?
<v Edward Dindinger>你知道有任何政策禁止议员与成年实习生发生关系吗？

00:41:11.290 --> 00:41:14.020
<v Representative>I've seen nothing in policy, no.
<v Representative>我没在政策中看到任何东西，没有。

00:41:14.020 --> 00:41:14.590
<v Edward Dindinger>Thank you.
<v Edward Dindinger>谢谢。

00:41:14.590 --> 00:41:23.710
<v Miki Meek>He's basically saying, tell me where it says a powerful, middle aged legislator can't date a 19-year-old intern, and always got the same answer.
<v Miki Meek>他基本上是在说，告诉我哪儿写着一个有权势的中年议员不能和一个19岁的实习生约会，得到的总是同样的回答。

00:41:23.710 --> 00:41:25.240
<v Representative 1>Written policy, no.
<v Representative 1>书面政策，没有。

00:41:25.240 --> 00:41:27.140
<v Representative 2>Not that I'm, not that I'm aware of.
<v Representative 2>据我所知没有。

00:41:27.140 --> 00:41:28.180
<v Representative 3>Absolutely not.
<v Representative 3>绝对没有。

00:41:28.180 --> 00:41:29.290
<v Edward Dindinger>OK.
<v Edward Dindinger>好的。

00:41:29.290 --> 00:41:50.920
<v Miki Meek>Von Ehlinger's lawyer did the same kind of thing with the other woman who testified about von Ehlinger's troubling behavior. Like a chief clerk who said one of her employees complained when von Ehlinger asked her out. His attorney's response to that was, she wasn't wearing a wedding ring, which of course, has nothing to do with whether von Ehlinger should have asked her out at work. His attorney charged ahead.
<v Miki Meek>冯·埃林格的律师对另一位作证关于冯·埃林格令人不安行为的女性也做了同样的事情。比如一位首席书记员，她说当冯·埃林格约她出去时，她的一名员工提出了投诉。他律师的回答是，她当时没戴结婚戒指，这当然与冯·埃林格是否应该在工作中约她出去毫无关系。他的律师冲在前面。

00:41:50.920 --> 00:41:58.700
<v Edward Dindinger>Ms. Maulin, in your experience, isn't wearing a wedding ring a generally accepted sign that a woman is unavailable?
<v Edward Dindinger>多林小姐，根据你的经验，没戴结婚戒指不是一个被普遍接受的女性处于单身状态的标志吗？

00:41:58.700 --> 00:42:02.850
<v Chief Clerk>It can be construed that way, but I don't think that that's a requirement to be unavailable.
<v Chief Clerk>它可以这样理解，但我不认为那是衡量是否单身的必要条件。

00:42:02.850 --> 00:42:19.010
<v Edward Dindinger>I didn't ask if it was a requirement. Is it completely unreasonable, in your opinion, for an individual who is interested in a woman to notice that she is not wearing a wedding ring and assume he's permitted to ask her a question?
<v Edward Dindinger>我没问那是否是必要条件。在你看来，一个对女性感兴趣的人注意到她没戴结婚戒指并假设他可以问她一个问题，这是完全不合理的吗？

00:42:19.010 --> 00:42:24.290
<v Chief Clerk>I don't think a wedding ring is indicative of whether or not you can ask a woman a question.
<v Chief Clerk>我不认为结婚戒指能说明你能否问一个女性问题。

00:42:24.290 --> 00:43:00.210
<v Miki Meek>This is an absurd thing to argue, but he's also arguing inside an absurd, incoherent set of rules. The Idaho legislature has a process for reporting sexual harassment, but not actually a policy against it in the rules for the Senate and the House. They don't have an anti-fraternization policy, but they have rules about decorum -- rules forbidding smoking, drinking, and eating on the House floor. A rule about conduct unbecoming of a state legislator, but nothing for sexual harassment.
<v Miki Meek>这是一种荒谬的辩论，但他也是在荒谬、不连贯的规则体系内进行辩论。爱达荷州立法机构有举报性骚扰的程序，但在参议院和众议院的规则中实际上没有禁止性骚扰的政策。他们没有反办公室恋情政策，但有关于礼仪的规则——禁止在众议院议事厅吸烟、饮酒和进食的规则。有一条关于州议员行为不端的规则，但没有关于性骚扰的规则。

00:43:00.210 --> 00:43:12.180
<v Miki Meek>The part of the hearing I'd always wanted to know more about ever since I first saw it was what happened when Jane finally walked away from that black curtain. She sat behind it for 40 minutes, answering questions.
<v Miki Meek>从我第一次看到那场听证会起，我就一直想了解更多细节，那就是“简”终于从那个黑布帘走开时发生了什么。她在后面坐了40分钟，回答问题。

00:43:12.180 --> 00:43:14.130
<v Sage Dixon>Thank you, Ms. Doe, you are excused.
<v Sage Dixon>谢谢你，多伊小姐，你可以退席了。

00:43:14.130 --> 00:43:24.300
<v Jane Doe>I freaking lose it. I lose it. And then I was just angry. I was like, I'm going to get the F out of here. I don't want to be here anymore. You think I want to help you?
<v Jane Doe>我简直要疯了。我要崩溃了。然后我只是生气。我心想，我要滚出这里。我不想再待在这儿了。你以为我想帮你？

00:43:24.300 --> 00:43:39.040
<v Miki Meek>Jane told me that when she stood up to leave, she had a panic attack. There was a private exit she was supposed to go out of, but she was so upset that she pushed through the wrong door and landed out in a public hallway. That's when that scream came out of her.
<v Miki Meek>“简”告诉我，当她站起来离开时，她惊恐发作了。本来有一个私人出口她应该从那儿出去，但她太难过了，推开了错误的门，最终走到了一条公共走廊里。那就是那声尖叫出来的时候。

00:43:39.040 --> 00:43:41.080
<v Miki Meek>[SCREAM]
<v Miki Meek>[尖叫声]

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:46.750
<v Jane Doe>Just start running up the hallway, and that's when I heard people behind me.
<v Jane Doe>开始沿着走廊跑，那是我就听到身后有人。

00:43:46.750 --> 00:43:57.040
<v Miki Meek>Some of von Ehlinger's supporters -- all women -- followed her out into the hallway. There was a local reporter with them, too. They crowded around her and started filming Jane with their phones.
<v Miki Meek>冯·埃林格的一些支持者——都是女性——跟着她进入了走廊。还有一个当地记者也跟着。他们围住她，开始用手机拍摄“简”。

00:43:57.040 --> 00:44:07.510
<v Jane Doe>I broke. I broke. I screamed. I screamed a lot. Like screaming how much I was going to kill myself. All I wanted to do was get to the top of the building.
<v Jane Doe>我崩溃了。我崩溃了。我尖叫。我尖叫了很多次。就像尖叫着说我要自杀。我只想跑到大楼的最高处。

00:44:07.510 --> 00:44:34.090
<v Miki Meek>She was trying to get to a balcony in the rotunda to throw herself off. Her lawyers blocked her. Jane collapsed on the ground. Her mom held her while her lawyers pulled out an umbrella and opened it to try and block her face from the people filming her. It took several minutes before security at the capitol finally showed up and escorted Jane out of the building.
<v Miki Meek>她正试图跑到圆形大厅的阳台跳下去。她的律师拦住了她。简瘫倒在地上。她的妈妈抱着她，律师撑开一把伞，试图挡住她的脸不让拍摄的人看到。过了几分钟，国会大厦的安保人员终于赶到，把“简”护送出了大楼。

00:44:34.090 --> 00:44:54.500
<v Miki Meek>The hearing lasted two days. At the end of the second day, the committee recommended to expel him. He resigned just hours later. Representative Dixon, head of the Ethics Committee, saw this as a successful outcome. I told Dixon how traumatizing the whole experience was for Jane, and what happened after she ran out of the hearing room.
<v Miki Meek>听证会持续了两天。第二天结束时，委员会建议驱逐他。几个小时后他就辞职了。道德委员会负责人迪克森议员认为这是一个成功的结果。我告诉迪克森整个经历对“简”来说有多大的创伤，以及她跑出听证室后发生了什么。

00:44:54.500 --> 00:45:03.980
<v Miki Meek>I mean, if that's what the consequences were for her for showing up in public -- for testifying in public, I mean, is it, yeah, was it worth having her be in public like that?
<v Miki Meek>我的意思是，如果那是她公开露面——公开作证的后果，我的意思是，是啊，让她那样公开作证真的值得吗？

00:45:03.980 --> 00:45:36.420
<v Sage Dixon>Probably not. But again, this was all new to us as a committee. Not being familiar with that trauma and how it expresses itself in people, we had no idea and did everything we thought we were doing correctly to protect her. But I think we could have gone on without that because we did have her previous testimony that we could have brought up and had or showed everybody. We had the transcripts that we were able to show to the body, which again, was important to me so that they knew what we had known. And that probably would have sufficed.
<v Sage Dixon>可能不值得。但同样，这对我们委员会来说都是全新的。不熟悉那种创伤以及它如何在人们身上表现出来，我们一点头绪都没有，并尽了我们认为正确的一切努力来保护她。但我认为我们可以不用那样做，因为我们有她之前的证词，我们可以提出来或展示给所有人看。我们有记录，可以展示给整个议会，这对我很重要，让他们知道我们所知道的。这可能就足够了。

00:45:36.420 --> 00:45:47.100
<v Miki Meek>This, of course, is impossible to know. Another member of the Ethics Committee told me they thought without Jane's testimony in person, von Ehlinger would still be in the legislature.
<v Miki Meek>这当然无法证实。道德委员会的另一名成员告诉我，他们认为如果没有“简”亲自作证，冯·埃林格依然会留在议会。

00:45:47.100 --> 00:45:49.380
<v Miki Meek>I mean, is there anything that you would want her to know or that you...
<v Miki Meek>我的意思是，有什么你希望她知道的，或者你……

00:45:49.380 --> 00:46:12.440
<v Sage Dixon>Just I'm very sorry if we increased that trauma or that we did, because it never was our intent. I know each one of those members feels very strongly that she was wronged and wanted to protect her, but just due to our inexperience, things didn't come out maybe as cleanly as they could have.
<v Sage Dixon>只是我非常抱歉，如果我们加剧了那种创伤，或者我们做了什么，因为那绝不是我们的本意。我知道每一位成员都强烈感受到她受到了冤枉，并想保护她，但由于我们的经验不足，事情可能没有像我们预期的那样处理得那么干净。

00:46:12.440 --> 00:46:39.550
<v Miki Meek>So what happened to Jane after all of that? She never finished the internship she loved at the capitol, and after the hearing, never went back to her office with her name in all caps. Five months after the hearing, she was cleaning houses and she was a wreck. She'd lost 20 pounds. She couldn't stop replaying the assault and the hearing in her head. So much of her day-to-day now was a physical battle. She was dealing with severe PTSD.
<v Miki Meek>那么在那之后“简”发生了什么？她再也没有完成她在国会大厦热爱的实习工作，听证会后，再也没回到那间名字全大写的办公室。听证会五个月后，她靠打扫房屋维生，而且整个人彻底垮了。她瘦了20磅。她无法停止在脑海里重温那场袭击和听证会。她现在的日常生活中很大一部分是一场身体的战斗。她患有严重的创伤后应激障碍（PTSD）。

00:46:39.550 --> 00:46:57.850
<v Jane Doe>Just going on the freeway and you would see the tip of the capitol, like my body would just, like [GASP]. Immediately. Just like when you're falling, can you imagine just that [GASP] moment? Like just staying in that. My body is just in a state of shock, you know? Like, frozen.
<v Jane Doe>只要上高速公路，看到国会大厦的顶端，我的身体就像会[喘气]。立即。就像你跌倒时，你能想象那种[喘气]时刻吗？就像停留在那里。我的身体处于休克状态，你懂吗？就像，僵住了。

00:46:57.850 --> 00:47:25.240
<v Miki Meek>She was still getting harassed by von Ehlinger sympathizers who continued to publish her name online. Random strangers had shown up at her old apartment, asking questions. There was even a man who'd show up at Boise political events, mocking her, dressed as a woman, wearing a sash with Jane's real name on it. There were also people who sympathized with her, too, and that was also hard. When she was out in public, she never knew when someone was going to try and approach her.
<v Miki Meek>她仍然受到冯·埃林格同情者的骚扰，他们继续在网上发布她的名字。随机的陌生人出现在她以前的公寓，问东问西。甚至有一个男人出现在博伊西的政治活动上，打扮成女人的样子，戴着写有“简”真名的绶带，嘲笑她。也有同情她的人，这也是一种折磨。当她在公共场合时，她从不知道什么时候会有人试图接近她。

00:47:25.240 --> 00:47:29.380
<v Jane Doe>Like people are like, are you Jane Doe?
<v Jane Doe>比如人们会问，你是简·多伊吗？

00:47:29.380 --> 00:47:32.060
<v Miki Meek>Where does this come up when you get recognized?
<v Miki Meek>当被认出来时，这话题会怎么引出来？

00:47:32.060 --> 00:47:56.040
<v Jane Doe>So I went to a job fair, OK, and it was just a bunch of booths of people, and you just go to the next one. And this one lady I went up to went, and I was like, you know, my name is [BLEEP]. I worked here and here, blah, blah, blah. I think I'd be, I'd like to take an application. And she's just like, her eyes were already watering. Like she was already, like, crying, and she's like, miss, did you, are you Jane Doe?
<v Jane Doe>我去了一个招聘会，好吧，那儿有一堆摊位，你只要走到下一个就行。我走到这个女士面前，我说，你知道，我的名字是[消音]。我曾在某某地方工作，巴拉巴拉。我想取一份申请表。然后她只是，她的眼睛已经湿润了。她已经，就像，在哭了，她说，小姐，你是简·多伊吗？

00:47:56.040 --> 00:47:57.120
<v Miki Meek>What did you say?
<v Miki Meek>你说了什么？

00:47:57.120 --> 00:48:35.750
<v Jane Doe>I was like, fuck. But I was like, yes. Yes, ma'am. Like, yeah. And she's like, oh my gosh. Like, I watched that, blah, blah, blah. And then I was recognized again at the Walmart, and that lady recognized me by my face. You know, and then that's when a lot of people try to share their frickin' rape stories, and you're just like, I really don't want to hear that. Like, I'm still recovering from my own. Like, why would you share that? So not that I don't care, per se, right? But I don't care. I don't want to hear it.
<v Jane Doe>我说，去他的。但我说，是的。是的，女士。就像，是啊。她说，天哪。就像，我看了那个，巴拉巴拉。然后我再次在沃尔玛被认出来，那位女士通过我的脸认出了我。你知道，然后很多人就试图分享他们该死的强奸故事，你只会觉得，我真的不想听那个。就像，我还在从我自己的经历中恢复。就像，你为什么要分享那个？不是说我不关心，对吧？但我就是不关心。我不想听。

00:48:35.750 --> 00:49:04.320
<v Miki Meek>Jane started talking about herself in the past tense. I was high energy. I was spunky. I was funny. And her plans felt past tense, too. She no longer talked about being a lawmaker. She was sure she could never set foot back in the capitol, and was thinking about moving out of state. The state house had a process to take her seriously when she reported von Ehlinger, but after that, they had nothing. Jane felt like they weren't concerned with what the experience did to her.
<v Miki Meek>“简”开始用过去时态谈论自己。我精力充沛。我生机勃勃。我很有趣。她的计划也感觉像是过去时了。她不再谈论成为一名立法者。她肯定自己再也不会踏入国会大厦，并考虑搬出州外。州议会有一个流程来认真对待她举报冯·埃林格的行为，但那之后，他们什么都没有。简觉得他们并不关心那种经历对她做了什么。

00:49:04.320 --> 00:49:40.070
<v Jane Doe>They just needed my testimony. They needed to do this because they needed to do their jobs. Like they didn't really look at me as a human. They were just, oh, well she reported this, so she got to do this. So might as well just fucking make her, right? Like that's what it felt like, that they only needed me to get this process going. Like they didn't consider anything else. And so I mean, that's where it gets me. Like I mean, what would you do, what would you have done if I was your kid? What would you have made sure happen if I was yours?
<v Jane Doe>他们只是需要我的证词。他们需要这样做是因为他们需要做好自己的工作。就像他们并没有真正把我当人看。他们只是，噢，好吧，她举报了这个，所以她得做这个。所以不如强迫她做，对吧？就像那种感觉，他们只用我来启动这个过程。就像他们没有考虑任何其他事情。所以我就是觉得难以接受。我的意思是，如果我是你的孩子，你会怎么做？如果我是你的孩子，你会确保发生什么？

00:49:40.070 --> 00:50:09.170
<v Miki Meek>All Jane had wanted to do was work at the capitol, but after she got doxxed, she said everyone there who talked to her about job opportunities went silent. To make money, she started helping her stepdad out at his plumbing business and picked up work as a caregiver at a memory care facility. And then one year after the ethics hearing, Jane got a rare sort of do over of the entire ordeal with von Ehlinger. The District Attorney in the County for Boise charged him with felony rape.
<v Miki Meek>“简”想要做的只是在国会大厦工作，但被“人肉”后，她说那里所有跟她谈论工作机会的人都沉默了。为了赚钱，她开始帮她继父的水管生意，还找了一份在记忆护理中心做护理员的工作。然后，在道德听证会一年后，“简”获得了一次罕见的关于冯·埃林格折磨她的重审机会。博伊西县地方检察官起诉他犯有重罪强奸。

00:50:09.170 --> 00:50:41.680
<v Miki Meek>This happened because after she got doxxed, Jane told the police she'd cooperate with the criminal case. She was so scared all the time, she felt like she had to for her own protection. This time, things went very differently. The prosecutors handling her case understood that they needed to deal with her with care. They walked her through each step so she always knew what to expect. They did not subpoena her. They intentionally built a whole case where she didn't have to take the stand. They had other witnesses like the nurse who did Jane's rape kit exam.
<v Miki Meek>这发生在被“人肉”后，“简”告诉警方她会配合刑事案件。她一直很害怕，觉得为了自己的保护她必须配合。这一次，情况进展非常不同。负责她案件的检察官明白他们需要小心对待她。他们带她完成了每一步，所以她总是知道会发生什么。他们没有传唤她。他们故意构建了一个她无需出庭的完整案例。他们有其他证人，比如为“简”做强奸取证的护士。

00:50:41.680 --> 00:51:21.900
<v Miki Meek>But Jane decided that she did want to testify against von Ehlinger. A Sheriff picked her up in an unmarked car and drove her to a secret courthouse entrance. She had a therapy dog named Yuko in the courtroom and a victim witness coordinator by her side. I was there, sitting a couple rows behind von Ehlinger, but even with all that support, watching Jane on the stand that day was harrowing. She looked like she was having an out-of-body experience. She stared at him blankly and could barely talk. When she tried to answer questions about how he'd raped her, she suddenly stopped. She pushed her chair away from the mic, stood up, and said, I can't do this. She walked out of the courtroom.
<v Miki Meek>但“简”决定她还是想指证冯·埃林格。一名治安官开着一辆便车把她送到法庭的秘密入口。她在法庭里有一只名叫Yuko的治疗犬，身边有一名受害者证人协调员。我就在那里，坐在冯·埃林格身后几排，但即使有所有这些支持，那天在证人席上看着“简”也是令人心碎的。她看起来像是灵魂出窍了。她呆滞地盯着他，几乎无法说话。当她试图回答关于他是如何强奸她的问题时，她突然停住了。她把椅子推离麦克风，站起来说，我做不到。她走出了法庭。

00:51:21.900 --> 00:51:48.680
<v Jane Doe>So I ran out of the room and immediately fell down a flight of stairs. Like that sounds stupid, but I did, and I don't know what, in my mind, like, I just needed to run, and then I ran across Front Street. I ran across Myrtle, which is the big road. Big, busy roads -- downtown Boise. Four lanes. I was trying to go to the bridge, OK. In my brain, that's where I was going, but my ankle was getting to me.
<v Jane Doe>所以我跑出了房间，立即摔下了一段楼梯。就像这听起来很愚蠢，但我确实摔了，我不知道为什么，在我的脑海里，我只是需要逃跑，然后我跑过了Front街。我跑过了Myrtle，那是条大路。又大又繁忙的马路——博伊西市中心。四车道。我试图去那座桥，好吧。在我的大脑里，那就是我要去的地方，但我的脚踝疼得要命。

00:51:48.680 --> 00:52:16.770
<v Miki Meek>The bridge, she says, because maybe she could jump. She was having a post-traumatic reaction. Same as at the hearing, Jane's first instinct was to do what she hadn't been able to do the night she was raped: run away. She'd torn a tendon and leaned up against a tree. One of her attorneys named Guy followed her out to try to calm her. Guy says when the police showed up, one of them grabbed her arm and dropped her to the ground. Jane was so distraught, she doesn't remember that.
<v Miki Meek>她说要去那座桥，因为也许她可以跳下去。她出现了创伤后反应。和听证会时一样，“简”的第一本能是做她在被强奸那天晚上没能做到的事：逃跑。她拉伤了肌腱，靠在一棵树上。她的一位律师盖伊（Guy）跟着她出来试图安抚她。盖伊说当警察出现时，其中一人抓住了她的手臂把她按倒在地。简当时太痛苦了，她不记得那件事了。

00:52:16.770 --> 00:52:29.440
<v Jane Doe>So many people. It was like nine different officers on me, and I was just on the floor. I was just screaming and, like, hitting my head over and over. And I looked over, and I'll never forget this, and I seen Guy on his hands and knees.
<v Jane Doe>那么多人。就像九个不同的警察压在我身上，我只是躺在地上。我只是尖叫，一直不停地撞自己的头。我看了看旁边，我永远不会忘记这一点，我看见盖伊跪在地上。

00:52:29.440 --> 00:52:30.480
<v Miki Meek>He was crying.
<v Miki Meek>他在哭。

00:52:30.480 --> 00:52:40.320
<v Jane Doe>And he was, like, shaking his head. And I don't know what about that hurt me, and I stopped for a second.
<v Jane Doe>他就像，摇着头。我不知道为什么那件事伤害了我，我停顿了一下。

00:52:40.320 --> 00:52:59.670
<v Miki Meek>During this brief pause, the police put a full body restraint wrap, a helmet, and handcuffs on Jane. They put her in a squad car and transferred her to a hospital for psychiatric care. She spent three days there. When she was released, her mom picked her up. As soon as they pulled on the freeway, a text showed up on her mom's phone.
<v Miki Meek>在这短暂的停顿中，警察给“简”戴上了全身约束带、头盔和手铐。他们把她放进警车并送往医院进行精神病护理。她在那里待了三天。出院时，她妈妈接上了她。当他们刚上高速公路时，她妈妈手机上弹出了一条短信。

00:52:59.670 --> 00:53:10.640
<v Jane Doe>I hear [DING SOUND] and then she, like [GASP]. She's like, do you want to read that? And I'm just like, OK. I have a smoothie in my hand, like...
<v Jane Doe>我听到[叮的声音]，然后她就像[喘气]。她说，你想读那个吗？我当时只是，好吧。我手里拿着奶昔，就像……

00:53:10.640 --> 00:53:16.250
<v Miki Meek>It was a text from one of Jane's attorneys. The jury in von Ehlinger's trial had just come to a verdict.
<v Miki Meek>那是来自“简”的一位律师的短信。冯·埃林格审判的陪审团刚刚达成了裁决。

00:53:16.250 --> 00:53:19.430
<v Jane Doe>And she said that he was found guilty.
<v Jane Doe>她说他被判有罪。

00:53:19.430 --> 00:53:21.980
<v Miki Meek>And what was, what did that feel like?
<v Miki Meek>那是什么感觉？

00:53:21.980 --> 00:53:26.090
<v Jane Doe>I didn't believe it. I was like, no way. What?
<v Jane Doe>我不相信。我心想，不可能。什么？

00:53:26.090 --> 00:53:27.350
<v Miki Meek>Why didn't you believe it?
<v Miki Meek>你为什么不相信？

00:53:27.350 --> 00:53:48.510
<v Jane Doe>Nobody thought that he would have, oh, just a slap on the wrist he's going to get because he's a good old boy or, there were so many comments. When do you get to hear about a prosecuted, convicted for what they did. And he got that. You know how lucky I am?
<v Jane Doe>没人会想到他会，因为他是个“好男孩”或者什么的，只会得到一点轻微的处罚，有很多评论。你什么时候听说过像他这样被起诉、被定罪的？他确实得到了惩罚。你知道我有多幸运吗？

00:53:48.510 --> 00:54:12.910
<v Miki Meek>It's true Jane is the best case scenario for what a rape survivor can get. Von Ehlinger was kicked out of the Idaho Capitol a month and a half after she reported. He was arrested, and a judge sentenced him to 20 years in prison. Less than 3% of sexual assaults ever result in a conviction or incarceration, but the lived reality of best case scenario for Jane hardly feels like a win.
<v Miki Meek>事实证明，“简”对于一个强奸幸存者能得到什么来说确实是最佳案例。冯·埃林格在她举报一个半月后被踢出了爱达荷州国会大厦。他被捕了，法官判处他20年监禁。只有不到3%的性侵案件最终导致定罪或监禁，但对于“简”来说，这“最佳案例”的现实生活几乎感觉不到是一场胜利。

00:54:12.910 --> 00:54:37.560
<v Miki Meek>Von Ehlinger still maintains he did nothing wrong. He's appealing his conviction to the state Supreme Court. His sympathizers continue to harass her, and she regularly sees a psychiatrist and a therapist for PTSD. She takes ice baths a few times a week to shock herself into feeling like she's back in her body.
<v Miki Meek>冯·埃林格依然坚持他没做错什么。他正向州最高法院上诉他的判决。他的同情者继续骚扰她，她定期去看精神科医生和心理治疗师以治疗PTSD。她每周洗几次冰水浴，通过冲击让自己感觉回到自己的身体里。

00:54:37.560 --> 00:55:00.250
<v Miki Meek>More than two years after the ethics hearing, nothing is preventing another Jane Doe from going through almost the exact same process she did. This really gets to her. The Idaho House and the Senate still have not adopted a sexual harassment or anti-fraternization policy into their rules. They also haven't adopted a specific process for how to investigate future reports of sexual misconduct.
<v Miki Meek>在道德听证会两年多后，没有任何事情能阻止另一个“简·多伊”经历她所经历的几乎完全相同的过程。这真的触动了她。爱达荷州众议院和参议院仍然没有在规则中采纳性骚扰或反办公室恋情政策。他们也没有为如何调查未来的性行为不端报告采纳特定流程。

00:55:00.250 --> 00:55:31.740
<v Miki Meek>There are state legislatures that have adopted some best practices. Oregon offers confidential counseling for victims to help them decide what kind of action they want to take, and New York and Oregon use independent external investigators. Neither state requires victims to retell and relive their assault in public. The one improvement Dixon and other Ethics Committee members did make is stronger confidentiality protections for anyone who reports, so anyone who releases a victim's name could face consequences.
<v Miki Meek>有些州立法机构已经采纳了一些最佳实践。俄勒冈州为受害者提供保密咨询，帮助她们决定采取什么行动，纽约州和俄勒冈州使用独立的外部调查员。没有一个州要求受害者在公开场合重述和重温她们的攻击经历。迪克森和其他道德委员会成员确实做出的一个改进是为任何举报人提供更强的保密保护，所以任何泄露受害者名字的人都可能面临后果。

00:55:31.740 --> 00:57:13.650
<v Miki Meek>But at the same time, the committee also decided they can cover the legal fees of lawmakers accused of sexual harassment or assault. This is where we are more than five years after #MeToo: more awareness there's a problem, a more immediate sense that you should care and respond when someone reports, but nobody's invented new systems where what a victim actually wants and needs is at the center.
<v Miki Meek>但与此同时，委员会也决定他们可以承担被控性骚扰或袭击的立法者的法律费用。这就是我们在“我也是”运动五年多后所处的境地：更多人意识到这是一个问题，有一种更强烈的紧迫感认为当有人举报时你应该关心并回应，但没有人发明新的制度将受害者真正想要和需要的放在中心。

00:57:13.650 --> 00:57:19.720
<v Jane Doe>Part of me was excited. Part of me, like, still admired the state house.
<v Jane Doe>我的一部分对此感到兴奋。另一部分，就像，仍然钦佩州议会。

00:57:19.720 --> 00:57:21.470
<v Miki Meek>What was the other part of you?
<v Miki Meek>你的另一部分是什么？

00:57:21.470 --> 00:57:35.760
<v Jane Doe>Flat just scared -- like confusion, like scared. I don't want to, I don't want to, I don't want to. Very scared. Like, I made sure I had running shoes on. I made sure I had, like, stretchy pants, you know.
<v Jane Doe>纯粹的害怕——就像困惑，就像害怕。我不想，我不想要，我不想要。非常害怕。就像，我确保自己穿上了跑鞋。我确保自己穿了那种弹性裤子，你懂吗。

00:57:35.760 --> 00:57:37.510
<v Miki Meek>So that you were ready to just...
<v Miki Meek>这样你准备好……

00:57:37.510 --> 00:57:46.330
<v Jane Doe>In case I need to run, like, just take precautions. I had somebody, you know, on standby in case I needed to get picked up.
<v Jane Doe>万一我需要跑，就像，只是采取预防措施。我有个人，你知道，在待命以防我需要被接走。

00:57:46.330 --> 00:58:04.450
<v Miki Meek>Now, more than two years out from the hearing, Jane's been back at the capitol more than a dozen times. She says sometimes she gets random stares from lobbyists or male representatives who won't look her in the eye, but she doesn't care. In January, she went to the inaugural ball held in the rotunda of the capitol.
<v Miki Meek>现在，离听证会已经两年多过去了，“简”已经回到国会大厦十几次了。她说有时会从游说者或男议员那里得到随意的注视，他们不愿看着她的眼睛，但她不在乎。一月，她参加了在国会大厦圆形大厅举行的就职舞会。

00:58:04.450 --> 00:58:05.620
<v Miki Meek>What was it like?
<v Miki Meek>那是什么样的？

00:58:05.620 --> 00:58:10.420
<v Jane Doe>It was fun. I mean, I didn't wear heels. I did still wear, like, flats just in case.
<v Jane Doe>很有趣。我的意思是，我没穿高跟鞋。我还是穿了平底鞋，以防万一。

00:58:10.420 --> 00:58:11.320
<v Miki Meek>Purposely?
<v Miki Meek>故意的？

00:58:11.320 --> 00:58:12.370
<v Jane Doe>Yeah.
<v Jane Doe>是。

00:58:12.370 --> 00:58:16.390
<v Miki Meek>She pulled up a video on her phone.
<v Miki Meek>她调出手机里的一段视频。

00:58:16.390 --> 00:58:18.520
<v Jane Doe>Look, there's the speaker.
<v Jane Doe>你看，那是议长。

00:58:18.520 --> 00:58:19.630
<v Miki Meek>The rotunda's all lit up.
<v Miki Meek>圆形大厅灯火通明。

00:58:19.630 --> 00:58:26.800
<v Jane Doe>Yeah, and it was, like, just all fancy. Like, it was just so cool. Then I danced. I danced with some people.
<v Jane Doe>是啊，而且它，就像，非常华丽。就像，太酷了。然后我跳舞了。我和一些人跳了舞。

00:58:26.800 --> 00:58:30.340
<v Miki Meek>A representative encouraged her to take a picture back on the House floor.
<v Miki Meek>一位议员鼓励她在众议院议事厅拍张照。

00:58:30.340 --> 00:58:37.990
<v Jane Doe>Literally I'm in the Speaker's spot in the House, holding the gavel. Just, she's like, pose, pose for me.
<v Jane Doe>字面意义上，我就坐在众议院议长的位置上，拿着木槌。她就像，摆姿势，为我摆姿势。

00:58:37.990 --> 00:58:43.150
<v Miki Meek>I, like, saw you two years ago and you were just like, I can't go back there. And now you've got a gavel.
<v Miki Meek>我两年前见过你，你还说，我不能回那儿去。现在你拿着木槌了。

00:58:43.150 --> 00:58:51.890
<v Jane Doe>I know, right? And I wish that, like, I wish things turned out differently.
<v Jane Doe>我知道，对吧？我希望，就像，我希望事情结果能有所不同。

00:58:51.890 --> 00:59:20.930
<v Miki Meek>Jane's thought about how different things might be, the alternate life she'd have if the rape had never happened. She'd still be working at the capitol instead of her stepdad's plumbing company. Maybe she'd be an attache, popping into committee meetings for things she wants to learn more about, like Health and Welfare or State Affairs. Instead, she's here -- somewhere between where she intended to be and the very dark place she's been in for the last couple of years. She's just now returning to her original plans.
<v Miki Meek>“简”想过如果事情会有多不同，如果强奸从未发生，她会有怎样的另一种人生。她仍然会在国会大厦工作，而不是在她继父的水管公司。也许她会成为一名专员，为了她想了解更多的事情跳进委员会会议，比如健康福利或州事务。相反，她在这里——介于她原本打算去的地方和过去几年她所处的非常黑暗的地方之间。她刚刚开始回到她最初的计划中。

00:59:20.930 --> 00:59:43.400
<v Jane Doe>I'm going to be a lawmaker. You know, I'll never be, I will never have shame walking into that building anymore. Going forward, hell no. Hell no. I'm a multi-generation Idahoan. You know, my mother is here. My siblings are here. My son is here. I'm here. No way you're going to oust me from, I pay my taxes. Like, this is our building.
<v Jane Doe>我要成为一名立法者。你知道，我以后走进那栋楼永远不会再有羞耻感了。展望未来，去他的。去他的。我是多代爱达荷人。你知道，我妈妈在这儿。我的兄弟姐妹在这儿。我的儿子在这儿。我在这儿。没人能把我赶出去，我交了税。就像，这是我们的楼。

00:59:43.400 --> 01:00:01.020
<v Miki Meek>Jane's mapping out her path back: PTA, city council, and then the state house.
<v Miki Meek>“简”正在规划她回去的路径：家长会、市议会，然后是州议会。

01:00:01.020 --> 01:00:33.030
<v Ira Glass>Miki Meek is a producer on our show. If you've experienced sexual assault and want to talk to somebody, you can reach out to the National Sexual Assault Hotline. 1-800-656-HOPE. If you're having thoughts of suicide, call the National Suicide Prevention Hotline. You do that by dialing 988 on your phone. That's it. Just 988. Fiona Apple recorded this for us.
<v Ira Glass>美纪·米克是我们节目的制片人。如果你经历过性侵并想找人聊聊，可以联系全国性侵犯热线。1-800-656-HOPE。如果你有自杀的想法，请致电全国自杀预防热线。只需在手机上拨打988。就是这样。只需988。菲奥娜·苹果（Fiona Apple）为我们录制了这段音乐。

01:00:33.030 --> 01:00:33.280
<v Ira Glass>["HERE WE HAVE IDAHO" BY FIONA APPLE]
<v Ira Glass>["这就是我们爱达荷" - 菲奥娜·苹果]

01:00:33.280 --> 01:01:16.200
<v Ira Glass>(SINGING) You've heard of the wonders our land does possess, its beautiful valleys and hills. The majestic forests where nature abounds, we love every nook and rill. And here we have Idaho, winning her way to fame. Silver and gold in the sunlight blaze, and romance lies in her name.
<v Ira Glass>(唱歌) 你听说过我们土地拥有的奇迹，它美丽的峡谷和山丘。自然资源丰富的雄伟森林，我们爱这里的每一个角落和溪流。这就是我们爱达荷，赢得她的成名之路。阳光下闪烁的金银，浪漫就在她的名字里。

01:01:16.200 --> 01:01:47.970
<v Ira Glass>Singing, we're singing of you, ah, proudly too, all our lives through we'll go singing, singing of you, singing of Idaho.
<v Ira Glass>唱歌，我们唱着关于你，啊，也自豪地唱着，我们将一生都在歌唱，歌唱着关于你，歌唱着爱达荷。

01:01:47.970 --> 01:02:54.010
<v Ira Glass>There's truly one state in this great land of ours where ideals can be realized. The pioneers made it so for you and me, a legacy we'll always prize. And here we have Idaho, winning her way to fame. Silver and gold in the sunlight blaze, and romance lies in her name. Singing, we're singing of you, ah, proudly too, all our lives through we'll go singing, singing of you, singing of Idaho.
<v Ira Glass>在这片伟大的土地上，确实有一个州能实现我们的理想。先驱者们为我们和我们自己创造了这一切，一个我们将永远珍视的遗产。这就是我们爱达荷，赢得她的成名之路。阳光下闪烁的金银，浪漫就在她的名字里。唱歌，我们唱着关于你，啊，也自豪地唱着，我们将一生都在歌唱，歌唱着关于你，歌唱着爱达荷。

01:02:54.010 --> 01:03:23.220
<v Ira Glass>OK, so I know that we could have ended the show right here with this song, and in fact, we did plan on ending it right here, but then last week, I did this one more interview that just got to those of us who were putting together the show, and it's an interview with somebody who Jane Doe has been thinking about. A woman who, for a while, was known as Emily Doe. And so here is just one more thing before we go today. This is Act Three. Doe Meet Doe.
<v Ira Glass>好的，我知道我们可以用这首歌结束节目，事实上，我们确实计划在这里结束，但上周，我又做了最后一次采访，这真的很触动我们这些制作节目的人，这是对简·多伊一直在想的一个人的采访。一个曾有一段时间被称为“艾米莉·多伊”的女性。所以今天结束前还有一件事。这是第三幕。多伊遇见多伊。

01:03:23.220 --> 01:03:40.910
<v Ira Glass>OK, so while Miki was talking to Jane for this story, Jane was reading this book called Know My Name, and Know My Name was written by a survivor who had been anonymous for several years, and then came out with her name. This is somebody who we had, actually, one quick quote from in the top of the show -- Chanel Miller is her real name.
<v Ira Glass>好的，当美纪为这个故事与“简”交谈时，“简”正在读这本书叫《Know My Name》，《Know My Name》是一个匿名了好几年，然后公开名字的幸存者写的。这是我们在节目开头引用的那个幸存者——香奈儿·米勒是她的真名。

01:03:40.910 --> 01:04:16.320
<v Ira Glass>Stanford swimmer Brock Turner sexually assaulted her in 2015, and then she wrote this victim impact statement as Emily Doe, and this statement went online. Millions of people read it. Eighteen members of the House of Representatives -- Democrats and Republicans -- took turns reading it out loud in Congress. It was a thing. And Jane Doe is thinking these days about whether she wants to come out with her real name, and so I talked to Chanel Miller about how she decided to do that, and also what it was like for her to be anonymous as Emily Doe for so long before she did.
<v Ira Glass>斯坦福大学游泳选手布洛克·特纳在2015年性侵了她，然后她以“艾米莉·多伊”的名字写了这份受害者影响声明，这份声明发到了网上。数百万人读了它。众议院的十八名议员——民主党和共和党人——轮流在国会大声朗读。那可是件大事。而简·多伊这些天正在考虑是否要公开自己的真实姓名，所以我与香奈儿·米勒谈了谈她是如何决定这样做的，以及在公开之前她匿名作为艾米莉·多伊这么长时间是什么感觉。

01:04:16.320 --> 01:04:51.800
<v Chanel Miller>In those four years, less than 10 people in my life knew, and at the time, it was cool that I had the power of invisibility. But long term, what's not cool is when you can't turn the invisibility off, because if you're always invisible, then you just start to slowly dissolve, and just to be in the world and leave such a big part of me at home every day led to such a fragmented experience by the end.
<v Chanel Miller>那四年里，我生命中不到10个人知道这件事，当时，能拥有隐身的力量很酷。但长远来看，当你无法关闭隐身功能时，那就不酷了，因为如果你总是隐形的，那么你只会开始慢慢溶解，而仅仅是身处世界，每天把这么大一部分自我留在家里，最终导致了如此碎片化的经历。

01:04:51.800 --> 01:04:57.050
<v Ira Glass>Did Emily Doe seem like a person who is separate from you in some way?
<v Ira Glass>艾米莉·多伊看起来像是一个与你某种程度上分离的人吗？

01:04:57.050 --> 01:04:57.560
<v Chanel Miller>Oh, yes.
<v Chanel Miller>噢，是的。

01:04:57.560 --> 01:04:58.700
<v Chanel Miller>[LAUGHTER]
<v Chanel Miller>[笑声]

01:04:58.700 --> 01:04:59.870
<v Chanel Miller>Yes.
<v Chanel Miller>是的。

01:04:59.870 --> 01:05:03.830
<v Ira Glass>Say more about that.
<v Ira Glass>多说点这个。

01:05:03.830 --> 01:05:33.740
<v Chanel Miller>Well, she's sort of like a beacon and she's very bold. Emily Doe is just this unshakable, clear headed person who didn't take anything from anybody. And I don't know. I am not confrontational. You have to understand, like, in my book, I talk about how when I'm handed change at the grocery store, I just throw it in my bag because I don't want to hold anybody up.
<v Chanel Miller>好吧，她就像一座灯塔，而且她非常大胆。艾米莉·多伊只是一个坚不可摧、头脑清晰的人，不接受任何人的东西。我不知道。我不喜欢冲突。你必须理解，比如在我的书里，我谈到了在杂货店找零钱时，我只是把它扔进包里，因为我不想耽误任何人。

01:05:33.740 --> 01:05:59.360
<v Chanel Miller>I get so nervous. If I'm at a cafe and I have to ask the barista the Wi-Fi password -- rather not. I'll figure out a way to work without internet for an hour. And so in my day-to-day life, I'm someone who is very shrunken most of the time, and it takes a lot to be able to assert myself. I think what's incredible about Emily Doe is that she can show up when she needs to.
<v Chanel Miller>我太紧张了。如果我在咖啡馆，我必须问咖啡师Wi-Fi密码——我宁愿不问。我会想办法在没有网络的情况下工作一小时。所以在我日常生活中，我是一个大多数时候都非常畏缩的人，需要很多努力才能坚持自我。我认为艾米莉·多伊不可思议之处在于她能在需要时出现。

01:05:59.360 --> 01:06:00.770
<v Ira Glass>Emily Doe takes a stand.
<v Ira Glass>艾米莉·多伊采取立场。

01:06:00.770 --> 01:06:01.400
<v Chanel Miller>Yes.
<v Chanel Miller>是的。

01:06:01.400 --> 01:06:04.790
<v Ira Glass>Emily Doe asks for the Wi-Fi password.
<v Ira Glass>艾米莉·多伊会问Wi-Fi密码。

01:06:04.790 --> 01:06:05.150
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHTER]
<v Ira Glass>[笑声]

01:06:05.150 --> 01:06:07.640
<v Chanel Miller>Yeah. Yes.
<v Chanel Miller>是的。是的。

01:06:07.640 --> 01:06:14.460
<v Ira Glass>And so you're reading about this Emily Doe, who seems so amazing. And it doesn't feel like you.
<v Ira Glass>所以你读到了关于这位艾米莉·多伊的事情，她看起来太棒了。而且这感觉不像是你。

01:06:14.460 --> 01:06:49.920
<v Chanel Miller>Emily Doe, I don't think, is allowed to make a lot of jokes. Like, she exists in a space that has that -- that can be sensitive, where you want to be really conscious of what you're saying. You want to take care of people. Even now, when people -- people will sometimes see me on the street and burst into tears, and I don't know what that is, but I just -- anytime somebody gives me a compliment, I imagine Emily Doe as this, like, statue, and they give me a flower, and I walk over and put the flower at her feet, and then I come back to myself.
<v Chanel Miller>我觉得，艾米莉·多伊不被允许开很多玩笑。就像，她存在于一个有那样的——敏感的空间里，你希望非常意识到你所说的话。你希望照顾别人。即使是现在，当人们——人们有时会在街上见到我然后突然大哭，我不知道那是什么，但我只是——每当有人给我赞美时，我就会想象艾米莉·多伊就像一尊雕像，他们给了我一朵花，然后我走过去把花放在她的脚下，然后回到我自己身上。

01:06:49.920 --> 01:07:01.470
<v Ira Glass>So the next thing I want to do is something unusual. Jane Doe from our story has read your book. She read it with interest and she sent these next questions.
<v Ira Glass>所以我想做的下一件事是很不寻常的。我们故事里的“简·多伊”读过你的书。她带着兴趣读了它，并发送了这些接下来的问题。

01:07:01.470 --> 01:07:01.920
<v Chanel Miller>OK.
<v Chanel Miller>好的。

01:07:01.920 --> 01:07:04.020
<v Ira Glass>That she would love to hear your answers to.
<v Ira Glass>她非常想听到你的回答。

01:07:04.020 --> 01:07:04.440
<v Chanel Miller>OK.
<v Chanel Miller>好的。

01:07:04.440 --> 01:07:23.800
<v Ira Glass>OK. First one. When did you know -- you can totally see where she's coming from in this question, too -- when did you know that you were emotionally and mentally ready to put your real name out there? What were the signs that you were seeing in your own life telling you that you were ready?
<v Ira Glass>好的。第一个。你什么时候知道——你完全可以看出她这个问题是从哪里来的——你什么时候知道自己在情感上和精神上准备好公开自己的真实名字了？你在自己的生活中看到的哪些迹象告诉你你准备好了？

01:07:23.800 --> 01:07:45.570
<v Chanel Miller>There were so many things I thought were impossible at the beginning that have now become ordinary, and I want her to know that for herself. So I couldn't even verbalize what had happened to me when I was alone in a therapist's office, and I wore a jacket.
<v Chanel Miller>开始时我觉得很多事情是不可能的，但现在已经变得普通了，我希望她也能为自己做到。所以我甚至无法在治疗室独自一人时说出发生在我身上的事，而且我当时穿了一件夹克。

01:07:45.570 --> 01:07:46.590
<v Ira Glass>This is at the beginning.
<v Ira Glass>这是在开始的时候。

01:07:46.590 --> 01:08:06.120
<v Chanel Miller>This is at the very beginning. I had wore a jacket with sleeves that covered my hands, and now I can walk in in a regular afternoon, make a recording that will go out to millions of people, and afterward, I will move on to something else.
<v Chanel Miller>这是在最开始的时候。我穿了一件袖子遮住我手的夹克，而现在我可以在一个普通的下午走进去，录制一段将发给数百万人的录音，之后，我会继续做别的事。

01:08:06.120 --> 01:08:08.010
<v Ira Glass>Recording -- you're talking about this interview we're doing right now.
<v Ira Glass>录音——你说的是我们现在正在做的这次采访。

01:08:08.010 --> 01:08:22.380
<v Chanel Miller>Correct, but this will not sink me, and by the end, I could get back on my feet so quickly, and so I thought, I don't have to be afraid anymore that something is going to blindside me.
<v Chanel Miller>没错，但这不会压垮我，最终，我可以很快地重新站起来，所以我心想，我不再害怕有什么会出其不意地打击我了。

01:08:22.380 --> 01:08:26.790
<v Ira Glass>And that's when you knew that you were emotionally and mentally ready to put your name out there?
<v Ira Glass>那就是你知道自己从情感和精神上准备好公开名字的时候？

01:08:26.790 --> 01:08:29.460
<v Chanel Miller>Yes, I can do this.
<v Chanel Miller>是的，我可以做到这一点。

01:08:29.460 --> 01:08:36.720
<v Ira Glass>Jane Doe's next question for you is, what's the pros and cons list you made before you came out with your own name?
<v Ira Glass>简·多伊给你的下一个问题是，你在公开自己名字之前列出的优缺点清单是什么？

01:08:36.720 --> 01:08:59.450
<v Chanel Miller>OK. Cons -- cons is that any time you are Googled, it is the first thing that comes up. It'll be the first thing everybody sees you as. Pros -- pros, I get to be in rooms with people again.
<v Chanel Miller>好的。缺点——缺点是每当你被谷歌搜索时，它就是第一个弹出来的东西。那将是每个人看到你的第一印象。优点——优点是，我又可以和别人待在同一个房间里了。

01:08:59.450 --> 01:09:00.590
<v Ira Glass>What does that mean?
<v Ira Glass>那是什么意思？

01:09:00.590 --> 01:09:13.580
<v Chanel Miller>Like, I don't have to be lonely anymore. I was so lonely. It's so lonely to be hiding this much. Like it's so isolating.
<v Chanel Miller>就像，我再也不用感到孤独了。我太孤独了。隐藏这么多太孤独了。就像这太孤立了。

01:09:13.580 --> 01:09:16.210
<v Ira Glass>Other cons?
<v Ira Glass>其他缺点？

01:09:16.210 --> 01:09:45.650
<v Chanel Miller>Feeling exposed. Like it felt like I was in this storm and I had a little tarp covering me, and at any moment, the wind was going to rip the tarp off, and I'm just clenching it down all of the time. And so when it finally rips off, you're like, OK, now I can just give in to it. I can get soaked and see where we go from here.
<v Chanel Miller>感到暴露。就像我待在风暴里，我有一块小防水布遮着我，在任何时刻，风都会把防水布撕开，而我一直在紧紧抓住它。所以当它最终撕开时，你会想，好吧，现在我可以顺其自然了。我可以被淋透，看看我们从这里去哪儿。

01:09:45.650 --> 01:09:50.720
<v Ira Glass>Are there any other pros or cons?
<v Ira Glass>还有其他优缺点吗？

01:09:50.720 --> 01:10:03.370
<v Chanel Miller>You know, it is hard -- like every time I'm introduced, there's a trigger warning, and I think that's, there'll probably be one at the top of this episode, and...
<v Chanel Miller>你知道，这很困难——就像每次我被介绍时，都有一个触发警告，我想那是，这个节目开头可能也会有一个触发警告，而且……

01:10:03.370 --> 01:10:06.920
<v Ira Glass>Wait, you mean every time you're introduced, you mean on the radio or something like that?
<v Ira Glass>等等，你是说每次你被介绍，你是说在广播或者类似的事情上？

01:10:06.920 --> 01:10:08.290
<v Chanel Miller>Or at an event.
<v Chanel Miller>或者在活动上。

01:10:08.290 --> 01:10:09.450
<v Ira Glass>At an event?
<v Ira Glass>在活动上？

01:10:09.450 --> 01:10:14.510
<v Chanel Miller>Yeah, this may be really distressing and I'm sitting there like, what am I about to do to these people?
<v Chanel Miller>是啊，这可能非常令人不安，而我坐在那里心想，我打算对这些人做什么？

01:10:14.510 --> 01:10:15.970
<v Chanel Miller>[LAUGHTER]
<v Chanel Miller>[笑声]

01:10:15.970 --> 01:10:24.560
<v Chanel Miller>So I want to be introduced and not have to warn people that I'm about to start speaking, you know?
<v Chanel Miller>所以我希望被介绍时不必警告人们我要开始说话了，你懂吗？

01:10:24.560 --> 01:10:26.420
<v Ira Glass>OK, this is our last question from Jane Doe.
<v Ira Glass>好的，这是我们简·多伊的最后一个问题。

01:10:26.420 --> 01:10:28.100
<v Chanel Miller>OK.
<v Chanel Miller>好的。

01:10:28.100 --> 01:10:37.600
<v Ira Glass>Who is Emily Doe to you now? Is she gone?
<v Ira Glass>艾米莉·多伊对你现在来说是谁？她走了吗？

01:10:37.600 --> 01:10:42.070
<v Chanel Miller>She's there. She'll definitely always be there.
<v Chanel Miller>她在那儿。她肯定会永远在那儿。

01:10:42.070 --> 01:10:44.560
<v Ira Glass>Be there as a separate person from you? Still?
<v Ira Glass>作为一个与你分开的人在那儿？依然？

01:10:44.560 --> 01:10:44.860
<v Chanel Miller>Yes.
<v Chanel Miller>是的。

01:10:44.860 --> 01:10:45.550
<v Ira Glass>Really?
<v Ira Glass>真的吗？

01:10:45.550 --> 01:10:48.220
<v Chanel Miller>Yeah. She's a statue.
<v Chanel Miller>是啊。她是一尊雕像。

01:10:48.220 --> 01:10:52.600
<v Ira Glass>Oh, I thought you might say that, like, you have now, you've integrated her. She's now part of you.
<v Ira Glass>噢，我以为你会说，就像，你已经现在，你已经整合她了。她现在是你的一部分了。

01:10:52.600 --> 01:10:58.990
<v Chanel Miller>Oh, yeah. I think in the beginning interviews, I said that because it sounds very harmonious.
<v Chanel Miller>噢，是啊。我想在最初的采访中，我确实那么说了，因为那听起来非常和谐。

01:10:58.990 --> 01:11:00.160
<v Chanel Miller>[LAUGHTER]
<v Chanel Miller>[笑声]

01:11:00.160 --> 01:11:01.820
<v Ira Glass>But it's not true.
<v Ira Glass>但这不真实。

01:11:01.820 --> 01:11:06.190
<v Chanel Miller>That the experience isn't great...
<v Chanel Miller>那种经历并不伟大……

01:11:06.190 --> 01:11:12.580
<v Ira Glass>OK. All right, noted. That's a lie. Let's move on. She's still out there.
<v Ira Glass>好的。好吧，记下了。那是个谎言。我们继续吧。她依然在外面。

01:11:12.580 --> 01:11:21.970
<v Chanel Miller>Yeah. I can summon her. (SIGH)
<v Chanel Miller>是啊。我可以召唤她。（叹气）

01:11:21.970 --> 01:11:42.660
<v Ira Glass>Chanel Miller. Chanel Miller's book, again, is called Know My Name. She's also a visual artist. She did the show art for today's episode that you can see at our website.
<v Ira Glass>香奈儿·米勒。香奈儿·米勒的书，再次重申，叫《Know My Name》。她也是一位视觉艺术家。她为今天的节目做了插图，你可以在我们的网站上看到。

01:11:42.660 --> 01:12:04.490
